257
00:09:41,282 --> 00:09:43,650
Oh, mama, you make it
sound so good.

258
00:09:43,683 --> 00:09:46,453
But here comes a herd
of performing zebras.

259
00:09:46,487 --> 00:09:50,123
There's probably nothing quite
like them this side of Africa.

260
00:09:50,157 --> 00:09:51,758
Each one
has been trained to...

261
00:09:51,792 --> 00:09:53,527
[All]
Ew!

262
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:54,795
... precise teamwork
at the crack

263
00:09:54,828 --> 00:09:56,230
of their driver's whip.

264
00:09:56,297 --> 00:09:59,433
We're getting into a whole
weird area here.

265
00:09:59,467 --> 00:10:02,737
Yes, it's "sexist
male fantasies on ice."

266
00:10:02,770 --> 00:10:04,038
[All laughing]

267
00:10:04,072 --> 00:10:07,742
♪♪ [Quacking along with music]

268
00:10:07,775 --> 00:10:09,877
You know, this is about when
Donnie and Marie come out.

269
00:10:09,910 --> 00:10:11,845
- Yep.
- Oh.

270
00:10:11,878 --> 00:10:14,348
I am not a zebra!

271
00:10:14,382 --> 00:10:16,150
♪♪

272
00:10:16,183 --> 00:10:17,518
[Quacking to the beat]

273
00:10:17,552 --> 00:10:20,988
And now, the little bareback
rider exults in her victory

274
00:10:21,022 --> 00:10:22,690
over the wild beasts.

275
00:10:22,723 --> 00:10:23,690
Uh-huh...

276
00:10:23,723 --> 00:10:25,025
And, incidentally,

277
00:10:25,058 --> 00:10:27,528
shows olympic skating form
as she does so.

278
00:10:27,562 --> 00:10:28,528
Ooh.

279
00:10:28,562 --> 00:10:31,332
I can do that!

280
00:10:31,365 --> 00:10:33,867
Well, she can do this
'cause she feels fresh.

281
00:10:33,900 --> 00:10:35,135
- Mm-hmm.
- Oh.

282
00:10:36,937 --> 00:10:40,340
[Quacking to the beat]

283
00:10:40,373 --> 00:10:43,810
This way to the kill floor.

284
00:10:43,843 --> 00:10:46,647
In their galloping finale,
the spritely creatures show

285
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:51,518
their pride in being the only
troop of trained zebras on ice.

286
00:10:51,552 --> 00:10:54,322
Yes, it's dehumanized
objectified circus on ice.

287
00:10:54,355 --> 00:10:56,757
The view changes as Jacqueline
du bief takes the spotlight.

288
00:10:56,790 --> 00:10:58,125
Jacqueline?

289
00:10:58,158 --> 00:10:59,927
One of the world's
great figure skaters,

290
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:02,262
the girl from Paris
tells the story

291
00:11:02,295 --> 00:11:04,165
of a Fawn's fight for life.

292
00:11:04,198 --> 00:11:05,833
What was she doing?

293
00:11:05,867 --> 00:11:08,502
The Fawn plays happily in
the dim forest without a care.

294
00:11:08,535 --> 00:11:11,838
Prelude to the afternoon
of a murder.

295
00:11:11,872 --> 00:11:15,608
♪♪

296
00:11:15,642 --> 00:11:17,612
You know, you guys,
there's nothing sadder

297
00:11:17,645 --> 00:11:19,180
than a gut-shot Fawn...

298
00:11:19,213 --> 00:11:20,247
- Hmm.
- Hmm.

299
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:21,848
On ice.

300
00:11:21,882 --> 00:11:23,183
Especially.

301
00:11:23,217 --> 00:11:25,887
Please, she's an artist,
do not throw pennies at her.

302
00:11:25,920 --> 00:11:27,220
Suddenly, hunters approach.

303
00:11:27,253 --> 00:11:29,657
Frantically, the Fawn
tries to escape.

304
00:11:29,690 --> 00:11:31,492
Suddenly, she darts
across the highway.

305
00:11:31,525 --> 00:11:33,027
[Screeching]

306
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:34,027
[Imitates gunshot]

307
00:11:34,062 --> 00:11:36,463
At last,
the hunters close in.

308
00:11:36,497 --> 00:11:38,565
There is no escape.

309
00:11:38,598 --> 00:11:41,235
Unshaven, stinky and boozed-up
on rumple minze,

310
00:11:41,268 --> 00:11:44,238
the hunters spray
bullets into the woods.

311
00:11:44,272 --> 00:11:46,107
Oh, mom, I don't want to--

312
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:48,008
I don't like
the circus on ice anymore.

313
00:11:48,042 --> 00:11:49,410
I want to go home!

314
00:11:49,443 --> 00:11:52,480
Shut up and watch the deer
get slaughtered, it's fun.

315
00:11:52,513 --> 00:11:54,215
Viscera is trailing
from her stomach.

316
00:11:54,248 --> 00:11:55,415
The meat will not be good.

317
00:11:55,448 --> 00:11:56,417
Venison!

318
00:11:56,450 --> 00:11:57,618
- Venison!
- Okay.

319
00:11:57,652 --> 00:11:59,120
[Both]
Venison! Ven...

320
00:11:59,153 --> 00:12:00,320
- ... ison.
- They shoot.

321
00:12:00,353 --> 00:12:02,623
- They score!
- The bullet finds its Mark.

322
00:12:02,657 --> 00:12:05,358
Aw, she skates over her own
intestines.

323
00:12:05,392 --> 00:12:06,560
Ms. Du bief illustrates

324
00:12:06,593 --> 00:12:08,562
the exquisite blend
of brilliant skating

325
00:12:08,595 --> 00:12:10,263
and interpretive dancing,

326
00:12:10,297 --> 00:12:12,365
which won her
the world's championship

327
00:12:12,398 --> 00:12:15,837
as she plays out the last
moments of the dying Fawn.

328
00:12:15,870 --> 00:12:18,172
And now a clown
will deliver her eulogy.

329
00:12:18,205 --> 00:12:20,007
There's always a spot
in a skating carnival

330
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:21,042
for the little fellows.

331
00:12:21,075 --> 00:12:22,610
But not in this show.

332
00:12:22,643 --> 00:12:24,878
... brave army of toy soldiers
is called out

333
00:12:24,912 --> 00:12:28,448
to deal with
the only dragon in captivity.

334
00:12:28,482 --> 00:12:30,885
Operation desert ice storm.

335
00:12:30,918 --> 00:12:32,520
♪♪

336
00:12:32,553 --> 00:12:35,688
The soldiers destroy
the delicate balance of nature.

337
00:12:37,290 --> 00:12:41,128
A volley of shots gravely fired
disintegrates the menace.

338
00:12:41,162 --> 00:12:44,532
Gravely?
That thing was grazing.

339
00:12:44,565 --> 00:12:46,333
Yeah, kill, heh.

340
00:12:46,367 --> 00:12:50,337
And now, the spotlight falls on
a world of delicate loveliness.

341
00:12:50,370 --> 00:12:52,205
- And kills them.
- The ballet.

342
00:12:52,238 --> 00:12:55,275
Spinning the substance
that dreams are made of,

343
00:12:55,308 --> 00:12:57,243
the beautiful ballerinas
of the ice

344
00:12:57,277 --> 00:13:00,147
create a world
of fairy make believe.

345
00:13:00,180 --> 00:13:03,350
Yeah, as long as you drop before
you came in the shower.

346
00:13:03,383 --> 00:13:05,385
Ballet skating is
a comparatively new development

347
00:13:05,418 --> 00:13:06,720
of the art.

348
00:13:06,753 --> 00:13:10,357
It requires all the grace
and control of the dancer

349
00:13:10,390 --> 00:13:14,695
combined with the rhythmic sweep
of the skater.

350
00:13:14,728 --> 00:13:18,565
The Emmett Kelly girls do
their famous spotlight routine.

351
00:13:18,598 --> 00:13:22,168
♪♪

352
00:13:22,202 --> 00:13:25,638
Vomit sprays out in a beautiful
technicolor dream.

353
00:13:25,672 --> 00:13:27,508
You know, we should
have worn our raincoats.

354
00:13:27,542 --> 00:13:29,510
Oh, yes.
They would bring--

355
00:13:29,543 --> 00:13:34,582
[coughing]

356
00:13:34,615 --> 00:13:38,918
The girls with the bruised legs
have to wear the long dresses.

357
00:13:41,222 --> 00:13:42,523
This little number
is named,

358
00:13:42,557 --> 00:13:46,560
"always a bridesmaid,
never a bride."

359
00:13:46,593 --> 00:13:50,163
Individual artistry is blended
into brilliant choreography.

360
00:13:50,197 --> 00:13:51,798
On Broadway, but not here.

361
00:13:51,832 --> 00:13:55,368
... etches its delicate patterns
on the glassy stage

362
00:13:55,402 --> 00:13:59,105
to interpret
a moonlight concerto on ice.

363
00:13:59,138 --> 00:14:01,075
With a filbert nut.

364
00:14:01,108 --> 00:14:03,910
- Every circus has its acrobats.
- Except this one.

365
00:14:03,943 --> 00:14:06,480
There's a thrill a second
in their breathtaking routines,

366
00:14:06,513 --> 00:14:08,915
and when it's
all done on skates,

367
00:14:08,948 --> 00:14:11,352
these daring young performers
are accomplishing feats

368
00:14:11,385 --> 00:14:15,755
even more difficult than many
an aerialist in a real circus.

369
00:14:15,788 --> 00:14:20,093
You know, even my mom
would be bored at this.

370
00:14:20,127 --> 00:14:22,963
♪ Torn between two skaters ♪

371
00:14:22,997 --> 00:14:24,332
♪ fee--♪
oh!

372
00:14:24,365 --> 00:14:26,233
Yes, it's "Jules and Jim"
on ice.

373
00:14:26,267 --> 00:14:28,835
As they perform
their death-defying spin,

374
00:14:28,868 --> 00:14:31,305
the girls' heads barely
miss the ice.

375
00:14:31,338 --> 00:14:34,173
Women are pulled apart
like fresh bread.

376
00:14:34,207 --> 00:14:38,712
♪♪

377
00:14:38,745 --> 00:14:41,215
It all adds up
to split-second artistry.

378
00:14:41,248 --> 00:14:42,950
At the civic center,
but not here.

379
00:14:42,983 --> 00:14:45,518
... the fiber of an athlete,
the precision of an juggler,

380
00:14:45,552 --> 00:14:47,553
and the nerve
of a test pilot.

381
00:14:47,588 --> 00:14:49,990
And the boredom
of an accordionist.

382
00:14:50,023 --> 00:14:54,795
Today's great skating carnivals
are as sophisticated as bop,

383
00:14:54,828 --> 00:14:58,298
as spectacular
as the boudoir boogie.

384
00:14:58,332 --> 00:14:59,533
And as dull as a beige room.

385
00:14:59,567 --> 00:15:02,703
This is like
watching paint dry.

386
00:15:02,737 --> 00:15:05,572
Oh, it's easy when you
sit down.

387
00:15:05,605 --> 00:15:07,273
♪♪

388
00:15:07,307 --> 00:15:09,843
Heh heh, girls.

389
00:15:09,877 --> 00:15:12,813
See if you can spot the men
in this chorus line.

390
00:15:14,247 --> 00:15:16,783
[Chuckling lecherously]

391
00:15:16,817 --> 00:15:19,787
Look, she's got
a sheep's butt.

392
00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:22,455
Ah.

393
00:15:22,488 --> 00:15:24,725
[Scatting]

394
00:15:24,758 --> 00:15:26,460
Suddenly,
mitosis takes place.

395
00:15:26,493 --> 00:15:29,630
The DNA strand separates
in a dazzling display of color.

396
00:15:29,663 --> 00:15:30,797
Hi, prophase!

397
00:15:30,830 --> 00:15:33,133
Hi, anaphase!

398
00:15:33,167 --> 00:15:35,168
I'm putting a stop to this.

399
00:15:35,202 --> 00:15:37,003
This is the age
of style in skating,

400
00:15:37,037 --> 00:15:38,972
of form as well
as acrobatic.

401
00:15:39,005 --> 00:15:40,740
Too bad it isn't the age
of talent.

402
00:15:40,773 --> 00:15:42,843
[Chuckles]

403
00:15:42,877 --> 00:15:46,047
Wow, really swingin', man.

404
00:15:46,080 --> 00:15:49,850
Hey, she's got a saggy diaper
that leaks.

405
00:15:49,883 --> 00:15:52,385
- Hope not.
- Yeah.

406
00:15:52,418 --> 00:15:54,455
♪♪

407
00:15:54,488 --> 00:15:56,290
Can you imagine
if you were the kind of person

408
00:15:56,323 --> 00:15:57,958
who enjoyed this?

409
00:15:57,992 --> 00:16:01,195
The syncopated rhythm
of the dance...

410
00:16:01,228 --> 00:16:02,628
... is not working tonight.

411
00:16:02,663 --> 00:16:04,565
New style, on skates.

412
00:16:04,598 --> 00:16:07,067
The practiced precision
of the pony chorus

413
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:10,037
brings new excitement
on steel.

414
00:16:10,070 --> 00:16:12,538
Yeah, taste my steel.
Uck.

415
00:16:15,775 --> 00:16:18,445
All the color and pageantry
of a stage revue...

416
00:16:18,478 --> 00:16:19,647
... is missing.

417
00:16:19,680 --> 00:16:21,582
And brought from behind
the footlights into

418
00:16:21,615 --> 00:16:24,418
the spotlights of the modern
skating carnival.

419
00:16:24,452 --> 00:16:27,120
The only consolation
is that they're all dead now!

420
00:16:27,153 --> 00:16:29,490
... for men and women
from every walk of life

421
00:16:29,523 --> 00:16:31,092
who come together
each year

422
00:16:31,125 --> 00:16:33,927
for the fun of building a newer
and more dazzling spectacle.

423
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:35,595
Oh, right, that's fun.

424
00:16:35,628 --> 00:16:37,397
As the flashing blades
twirl to a dizzy climax,

425
00:16:37,430 --> 00:16:40,300
the skaters bring to an end
no ordinary circus,

426
00:16:40,333 --> 00:16:43,437
but truly a circus on ice.

427
00:16:43,470 --> 00:16:45,938
By federico fellini.

428
00:16:45,972 --> 00:16:49,377
We say good-bye to the vibrating
circus train.

429
00:16:49,410 --> 00:16:54,047
[Gurgling]

430
00:17:02,488 --> 00:17:03,423
Ah.

431
00:17:03,457 --> 00:17:04,458
♪♪

432
00:17:04,492 --> 00:17:06,327
Hey, I know this.

433
00:17:06,360 --> 00:17:10,763
[Humming theme
to "Hawaii five-o"]

434
00:17:10,797 --> 00:17:12,867
No, no, that's not it here.

435
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:15,368
- Oh, I know.
- Wipeout!

436
00:17:15,402 --> 00:17:17,537
[Humming "wipe out"]

437
00:17:17,570 --> 00:17:18,672
No, no, I don't--

438
00:17:18,705 --> 00:17:20,740
Elvis has staggered
out of the building.

439
00:17:20,773 --> 00:17:24,612
It's Billy pilgrim.

440
00:17:24,645 --> 00:17:26,680
Oh, that's a new L.A. club.

441
00:17:26,713 --> 00:17:27,915
Ohh...

442
00:17:27,948 --> 00:17:29,215
"Monster a-go go"?

443
00:17:29,248 --> 00:17:31,050
I thought this was gonna be
"munster, go home."

444
00:17:31,085 --> 00:17:32,652
- Ohh.
- You know what, guys?

445
00:17:32,685 --> 00:17:34,922
I have a feeling this is gonna
be a tough one.

446
00:17:34,955 --> 00:17:36,823
No, it might
not be too bad.

447
00:17:36,857 --> 00:17:38,158
No, I think Joel's right.

448
00:17:38,192 --> 00:17:39,860
This one has stinkburger
written all over it.

449
00:17:39,893 --> 00:17:41,462
- Yes.
- [Sighs]

450
00:17:41,495 --> 00:17:44,330
Now, come on, you can't tell
just from the credits.

451
00:17:44,363 --> 00:17:46,300
No, no,
it's a feeling I have.

452
00:17:46,333 --> 00:17:49,970
My gut instincts tell me this
is gonna hurt real bad.

453
00:17:50,003 --> 00:17:51,972
Joel, it's not healthy
to have such a negative attitude

454
00:17:52,005 --> 00:17:52,973
right out of the gate.

455
00:17:53,007 --> 00:17:54,340
It's just common sense,
crow.

456
00:17:54,373 --> 00:17:55,342
There's a feeling
of incompetence

457
00:17:55,375 --> 00:17:57,310
already in the air here.

458
00:17:57,343 --> 00:17:59,880
You know, we might as well
face up to it.

459
00:17:59,913 --> 00:18:01,448
Well, I refuse
to give in so soon.

460
00:18:01,482 --> 00:18:05,252
I'm gonna riff away
like it's nobody's business.

461
00:18:05,285 --> 00:18:07,587
I can't think of anything now,
but...

462
00:18:07,620 --> 00:18:09,022
[Chuckles]

463
00:18:09,055 --> 00:18:11,023
Well.

464
00:18:11,057 --> 00:18:12,692
"The other 'thuh'"?

465
00:18:12,725 --> 00:18:14,093
"Thh." the other "thh."

466
00:18:14,127 --> 00:18:15,462
- "Thh."
- "thh."

467
00:18:15,495 --> 00:18:17,430
♪♪

468
00:18:17,463 --> 00:18:19,298
Jeff Smith,
the frugal screenwriter!

469
00:18:19,332 --> 00:18:22,702
Now, don't write in,
for heaven's sake.

470
00:18:22,735 --> 00:18:24,838
♪♪

471
00:18:24,872 --> 00:18:26,873
Hey, didn't
Sheldon Seymour write

472
00:18:26,907 --> 00:18:28,242
"the other side of midnight"?

473
00:18:28,275 --> 00:18:30,277
Uh... hmm.

474
00:18:30,310 --> 00:18:31,712
Hey, what a coincidence.

475
00:18:31,745 --> 00:18:33,247
There are two guys
named bill rebane.

476
00:18:33,280 --> 00:18:35,148
Heh, you know, 'cause you
see with the--

477
00:18:35,182 --> 00:18:36,417
and the guys, they're--

478
00:18:36,450 --> 00:18:38,118
this is gonna suck.

479
00:18:38,152 --> 00:18:41,355
What you're about to see
may not even be possible

480
00:18:41,388 --> 00:18:44,558
within the narrow limits
of human understanding.

481
00:18:44,592 --> 00:18:46,293
So let's forget it
right now.

482
00:18:46,327 --> 00:18:49,362
A space capsule is rocketed
into orbit on schedule.

483
00:18:49,395 --> 00:18:51,632
[Humming "ride of
the valkyries"]

484
00:18:51,665 --> 00:18:55,302
Its mission, to observe
new objects circling the earth,

485
00:18:55,335 --> 00:18:58,905
satellites which no nation
has launched.

486
00:18:58,938 --> 00:19:01,140
As the capsule
reached its orbit,

487
00:19:01,175 --> 00:19:03,843
communications with it
suddenly went silent.

488
00:19:03,877 --> 00:19:06,480
Several days passed.

489
00:19:06,513 --> 00:19:09,448
A search team headed
by colonel Steve Connors...

490
00:19:09,482 --> 00:19:10,783
[All]
Hi, Steve!

491
00:19:10,817 --> 00:19:13,920
... an intensive search
of the entire area.

492
00:19:13,953 --> 00:19:15,388
This is Connors.

493
00:19:15,422 --> 00:19:17,057
Go ahead, patrol two.

494
00:19:17,090 --> 00:19:18,592
Go ahead,
pump three.

495
00:19:18,625 --> 00:19:21,462
Nothing in sight
except trees.

496
00:19:21,495 --> 00:19:23,397
Coming in, over.

497
00:19:23,430 --> 00:19:26,400
Um, I'll need extra Jack sauce
and some ketchup.

498
00:19:26,433 --> 00:19:27,767
Over and out.

499
00:19:27,800 --> 00:19:30,637
In a wooded area not far
from the space agency

500
00:19:30,670 --> 00:19:33,440
astrophysical laboratories
in Chicago,

501
00:19:33,473 --> 00:19:36,843
observers reported
that a strange object

502
00:19:36,877 --> 00:19:37,843
had fallen to earth.

503
00:19:37,878 --> 00:19:39,747
A coke bottle.

504
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:41,682
Patrol two to Connors.
Come in, over.

505
00:19:41,715 --> 00:19:43,117
Okay, now you see that thing?

506
00:19:43,150 --> 00:19:45,918
You push it down, all the way
down to the floor there.

507
00:19:45,952 --> 00:19:47,187
This is Connors.

508
00:19:47,220 --> 00:19:48,288
Come in, patrol two.

509
00:19:48,322 --> 00:19:49,957
Nag, nag, nag.

510
00:19:49,990 --> 00:19:51,190
I just spotted something.

511
00:19:51,225 --> 00:19:53,193
Looks like it
might be our stray man.

512
00:19:53,227 --> 00:19:55,395
I'm going down there
to take a look.

513
00:19:55,428 --> 00:19:57,932
Hey, good lookin', we'll be back
to pick you up later.

514
00:19:57,965 --> 00:19:59,232
They may have
found something.

515
00:19:59,265 --> 00:20:00,700
Was it the space capsule?

516
00:20:00,733 --> 00:20:02,535
You have five seconds
to answer.

517
00:20:02,568 --> 00:20:03,837
Ding! Ding!

518
00:20:03,870 --> 00:20:05,638
Ding!
[Imitating buzzer]

519
00:20:05,672 --> 00:20:08,175
I'm sorry,
the answer was no.

520
00:20:08,208 --> 00:20:10,710
Traffic is heavy
on the outbound 94.

521
00:20:10,743 --> 00:20:12,445
You might want to take
an alternate route.

522
00:20:12,478 --> 00:20:14,147
How does it look, Jim?

523
00:20:14,180 --> 00:20:15,948
This is so cool.

524
00:20:15,982 --> 00:20:17,985
Hey, wait a minute.

525
00:20:18,018 --> 00:20:19,418
That's right, this is it.

526
00:20:19,452 --> 00:20:22,555
Take the kazoo
out of your mouth.

527
00:20:22,588 --> 00:20:24,223
Yeah, that's it all right.

528
00:20:24,257 --> 00:20:26,727
Looks pretty battered
from here.

529
00:20:26,760 --> 00:20:28,562
I'm setting it down.

530
00:20:28,595 --> 00:20:30,397
Good-bye, Steve!

531
00:20:30,430 --> 00:20:36,570
[All humming "m a s h" theme]

532
00:20:36,603 --> 00:20:37,903
I like it.

533
00:20:37,938 --> 00:20:39,138
It's nice
they shot this film

534
00:20:39,172 --> 00:20:40,907
at such a pretty time
of the year, huh?

535
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:43,877
Mm-hmm.

536
00:20:43,910 --> 00:20:45,245
Ha.

537
00:20:45,278 --> 00:20:48,515
Well, I guess this means
the helicopter's landing.

538
00:20:48,548 --> 00:20:49,850
Yep.

539
00:20:49,883 --> 00:20:51,385
- Aah.
- Here it goes.

540
00:20:51,418 --> 00:20:54,320
Landing.

541
00:20:54,353 --> 00:20:55,822
I didn't know
what to say, Steve.

542
00:20:55,855 --> 00:20:57,257
I didn't know you felt
this way.

543
00:20:57,290 --> 00:20:58,492
Oh.

544
00:20:58,525 --> 00:21:00,727
Wait a minute.
There's an alien inside.

545
00:21:00,760 --> 00:21:04,163
They're talking
to Charlie brown's mom.

546
00:21:04,197 --> 00:21:05,698
Oh, my god!

547
00:21:05,732 --> 00:21:07,167
[Groaning]

548
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:08,468
He chucked, Steve.

549
00:21:08,502 --> 00:21:11,003
This is captain Connors
calling patrol two.

550
00:21:11,037 --> 00:21:12,705
Connors calling patrol two.

551
00:21:12,738 --> 00:21:14,775
He's not home right now,
can I take a message?

552
00:21:14,808 --> 00:21:17,577
Jim, do you read me?

553
00:21:17,610 --> 00:21:18,845
Come in,
patrol two.

554
00:21:18,878 --> 00:21:20,547
Wayne, pick up,
I know you're there.

555
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:22,015
What happened?

556
00:21:22,048 --> 00:21:24,450
It sounded like
he was in trouble.

557
00:21:24,483 --> 00:21:25,585
No duh.

558
00:21:25,618 --> 00:21:26,920
We better take a look.

559
00:21:28,922 --> 00:21:29,890
Hmm.

560
00:21:29,923 --> 00:21:31,858
Uh, take a left
by that bush.

561
00:21:33,393 --> 00:21:35,362
So the government
drives stock chevys?

562
00:21:35,395 --> 00:21:36,497
I think it's a biscayne.

563
00:21:36,530 --> 00:21:38,698
Ooh. Ooh.

564
00:21:38,732 --> 00:21:41,635
Oh, yeah, this is when
NASA was just a car

565
00:21:41,668 --> 00:21:43,003
and a helicopter, right?

566
00:21:43,037 --> 00:21:45,672
Mm-hmm, yeah.
Storefront in a strip mall.

567
00:21:45,705 --> 00:21:48,342
Yeah,
and a vacant lot.

568
00:21:48,375 --> 00:21:50,510
Okay, now, cut that out!

569
00:21:50,543 --> 00:21:53,147
Without question,
this was the capsule

570
00:21:53,180 --> 00:21:55,048
that had put Douglas
into orbit.

571
00:21:55,082 --> 00:21:56,517
- Right.
- [Laughing]

572
00:21:56,550 --> 00:21:59,385
And, without question, Douglas
was gone without a trace.

573
00:21:59,418 --> 00:22:03,257
Douglas was pear-shaped, very
short, and stood the whole way.

574
00:22:03,290 --> 00:22:05,058
[Giggling]

575
00:22:05,092 --> 00:22:07,693
Well, ray, look, it's filled
with schlitz cans.

576
00:22:07,727 --> 00:22:10,097
Oh, for crying out loud...

577
00:22:10,130 --> 00:22:14,133
Well, thank goodness les Paul
is backing them up on guitar.

578
00:22:14,167 --> 00:22:16,570
♪♪

579
00:22:16,603 --> 00:22:19,405
Earl, it's not
your break yet.

580
00:22:19,438 --> 00:22:22,408
The helicopter pilot,
who had discovered the capsule,

581
00:22:22,442 --> 00:22:25,045
was dead, horribly
mangled in a way

582
00:22:25,078 --> 00:22:27,047
no one had ever seen before.

583
00:22:27,080 --> 00:22:30,317
Oh, quit playing
with his face, Aaron.

584
00:22:30,350 --> 00:22:31,485
[Doorbell rings]

585
00:22:31,518 --> 00:22:34,020
Meanwhile, in a soap opera
not far away...

586
00:22:36,990 --> 00:22:38,392
What do you want?

587
00:22:38,425 --> 00:22:40,527
Well, Laura, how are you?

588
00:22:40,560 --> 00:22:41,928
How's Jimmy?

589
00:22:41,962 --> 00:22:44,765
Oh, he's fine, I just sent him
over to Mrs. Williams.

590
00:22:44,798 --> 00:22:47,400
- Hi, honey.
- Air kiss.

591
00:22:49,435 --> 00:22:50,837
Have some coffee?

592
00:22:50,870 --> 00:22:52,005
No, no thanks.

593
00:22:52,038 --> 00:22:53,005
Tom?

594
00:22:53,038 --> 00:22:54,007
Yes, please.

595
00:22:54,040 --> 00:22:55,875
Make it good this time!

596
00:22:55,908 --> 00:22:59,278
Huh, she usually keeps a bunch
of m&ms in that bowl.

597
00:22:59,312 --> 00:23:00,347
Hmm.

598
00:23:00,380 --> 00:23:02,948
Oh, feel free to stink
the house up.

599
00:23:02,982 --> 00:23:04,150
Jeez.

600
00:23:04,183 --> 00:23:05,518
Well, here's
the witch's brew.

601
00:23:05,552 --> 00:23:07,520
I mean, coffee, coffee.

602
00:23:07,553 --> 00:23:12,592
♪ Times like these were made
for taster's choice ♪

603
00:23:12,625 --> 00:23:14,060
It's about frank,
isn't it?

604
00:23:14,093 --> 00:23:16,262
[Imitating spit take]

605
00:23:16,295 --> 00:23:18,232
Ruth, the capsule came back.

606
00:23:18,265 --> 00:23:20,400
This coffee's warmer
than frank is.

607
00:23:20,433 --> 00:23:21,735
What about frank?

608
00:23:21,768 --> 00:23:23,035
I don't know.

609
00:23:23,068 --> 00:23:25,572
He had all the emergency
equipment he needed, but...

610
00:23:25,605 --> 00:23:27,273
He was just stupid.

611
00:23:27,307 --> 00:23:28,475
Ohh.

612
00:23:28,508 --> 00:23:30,243
Oh, what is it all about?

613
00:23:30,277 --> 00:23:32,378
- Alfie.
- The shot was a success.

614
00:23:32,412 --> 00:23:33,480
The capsule
did come back.

615
00:23:33,513 --> 00:23:34,982
But frank didn't.

616
00:23:35,015 --> 00:23:36,348
It's just that
we don't know whether

617
00:23:36,382 --> 00:23:38,152
the radiation repellent
worked or not.

618
00:23:38,185 --> 00:23:40,320
Oh, I know I shouldn't
feel this way.

619
00:23:40,353 --> 00:23:43,790
It's his life,
but... well...

620
00:23:43,823 --> 00:23:46,727
Ever since Harry died, frank's
been like a father to Jimmy.

621
00:23:46,760 --> 00:23:48,495
Harry?
Wait, wait, slow down.

622
00:23:48,528 --> 00:23:50,830
Try not to even think about it
right now.

623
00:23:50,863 --> 00:23:51,832
What am I going
to tell Jimmy?

624
00:23:51,865 --> 00:23:52,932
Who's Jimmy?

625
00:23:52,965 --> 00:23:54,200
Would you like me
to spend the night?

626
00:23:54,233 --> 00:23:57,303
Oh, you'd like that,
wouldn't you?

627
00:23:57,337 --> 00:23:58,905
I've got to get back
to the lab room.

628
00:23:58,938 --> 00:24:00,207
I don't want to spend
any more time on this

629
00:24:00,240 --> 00:24:01,440
than I already have.

630
00:24:01,475 --> 00:24:03,177
- If there's anything I can do.
- Thank you, Carl.

631
00:24:03,210 --> 00:24:05,012
Course, we'll be cutting off
the pension,

632
00:24:05,045 --> 00:24:08,448
- you understand.
- Mm-hmm.

633
00:24:08,482 --> 00:24:10,183
Hi, Dr. Carl.

634
00:24:10,217 --> 00:24:12,152
- Hiya, space cadet.
- Frank's dead!

635
00:24:12,185 --> 00:24:13,820
Jimmy, I thought you were over
at Mrs. Williams.

636
00:24:13,853 --> 00:24:16,388
I had to get
some more rocket parts.

637
00:24:16,422 --> 00:24:17,557
Poor kid.

638
00:24:17,590 --> 00:24:19,325
When's uncle frank
coming home?

639
00:24:19,358 --> 00:24:21,495
You'll have to make do
with uncle Bob and uncle Jim

640
00:24:21,528 --> 00:24:23,697
and uncle Phil and uncle Roy
and uncle Tim and...

641
00:24:23,730 --> 00:24:25,032
Remember he went
on a long trip?

642
00:24:25,065 --> 00:24:26,098
Burned on reentry, huh?

643
00:24:26,132 --> 00:24:27,367
He promised
to take me fishing.

644
00:24:27,400 --> 00:24:29,102
Well, some trips are longer
than we expected.

645
00:24:29,135 --> 00:24:30,470
Make other plans,
Jimmy.

646
00:24:30,503 --> 00:24:32,205
What's wrong with mommy?

647
00:24:32,238 --> 00:24:33,873
Mommy's not feeling
very well.

648
00:24:33,907 --> 00:24:35,475
Suppose you go upstairs,
and when we're all through,

649
00:24:35,508 --> 00:24:37,410
we'll have a nice big
ice cream soda, huh?

650
00:24:37,443 --> 00:24:40,647
Yeah, that solves everything.

651
00:24:40,680 --> 00:24:42,448
Hey, Timmy,
my dad's dead again!

652
00:24:42,482 --> 00:24:43,750
Can I come over for dinner?

653
00:24:43,783 --> 00:24:45,718
No, no, I can't take anymore.

654
00:24:45,752 --> 00:24:47,520
Oh, look, let's not make
a bigger deal out of this

655
00:24:47,553 --> 00:24:49,222
than it really is.

656
00:24:49,255 --> 00:24:50,557
[Phone rings]
[All] Aah!

657
00:24:50,590 --> 00:24:51,592
Whew.

658
00:24:51,625 --> 00:24:53,260
Jeez.

659
00:24:53,293 --> 00:24:55,128
Got that?
Face like spinach dip?

660
00:24:55,162 --> 00:24:56,128
Horribly mangled?

661
00:24:56,162 --> 00:24:57,697
No dental records?

662
00:24:57,730 --> 00:24:59,298
Hmm, too bad.

663
00:24:59,332 --> 00:25:01,000
All right, now.
I'll be right over.

664
00:25:01,033 --> 00:25:03,737
Well, looks like he died
in a state of sin.

665
00:25:03,770 --> 00:25:05,805
- Oh, sorry.
- They've located the cabin.

666
00:25:05,838 --> 00:25:07,607
- Two words, "closed casket."
- Is it frank?

667
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:12,045
I'll let you know
as soon as I find out.

668
00:25:12,078 --> 00:25:14,480
Wait, Carl,
I want to go with you.

669
00:25:14,513 --> 00:25:17,150
Honey, he'll call if there
is any news.

670
00:25:17,183 --> 00:25:19,085
No, I want to be with you.

671
00:25:19,118 --> 00:25:20,620
[Sighs and hums]

672
00:25:20,653 --> 00:25:22,288
So sad.

673
00:25:22,322 --> 00:25:25,592
♪ Hum de-Dee hee hee,
hoo-ah hoo-ah ♪

674
00:25:37,670 --> 00:25:40,473
♪ Hum de-Dee hee hee,
hoo-ah hoo-ah ♪

675
00:25:40,507 --> 00:25:42,242
♪ hum de-Dee hee hee ♪

676
00:25:42,275 --> 00:25:43,277
♪ hoo-ah hoo-ah ♪

677
00:25:43,310 --> 00:25:44,277
♪ hum-- ♪

678
00:25:44,310 --> 00:25:45,878
- Hi!
- Hi.

679
00:25:45,912 --> 00:25:47,880
♪ Di Dee hee hee,
hoo-ah hoo-ah ♪

680
00:25:47,913 --> 00:25:50,917
Um... uh...

681
00:25:50,950 --> 00:25:52,252
Uh...

682
00:25:53,553 --> 00:25:55,388
Oh. Hmm.

683
00:25:55,422 --> 00:25:56,790
Crow?

684
00:25:56,823 --> 00:25:57,890
Yes?

685
00:25:57,923 --> 00:25:59,858
I don't get you.

686
00:25:59,892 --> 00:26:00,927
Oh.

687
00:26:00,960 --> 00:26:01,895
- Are you mad?

688
00:26:01,928 --> 00:26:03,195
No! No.

689
00:26:03,228 --> 00:26:04,297
Good!

690
00:26:04,330 --> 00:26:05,798
Because I want to like you,

691
00:26:05,832 --> 00:26:07,900
but I just don't understand
where you're coming from.

692
00:26:07,933 --> 00:26:09,768
Sure, uh...

693
00:26:09,802 --> 00:26:11,370
What's not to get,
though, gypsy?

694
00:26:11,403 --> 00:26:13,238
I just am, I hang out.

695
00:26:13,273 --> 00:26:14,740
Oh, oh, I know.

696
00:26:14,773 --> 00:26:19,145
It's just that, well, you know,
I don't really get you.

697
00:26:19,178 --> 00:26:20,913
Yeah, okay.

698
00:26:20,947 --> 00:26:22,782
Uh, maybe I can help.

699
00:26:22,815 --> 00:26:25,818
Uh, to start with,
I'm a robot.

700
00:26:25,852 --> 00:26:27,553
I use
cyber-based bubble memory.

701
00:26:27,587 --> 00:26:29,122
Is that what's
confusing you?

702
00:26:29,155 --> 00:26:30,957
N-No.

703
00:26:30,990 --> 00:26:33,158
Is it that I work off unix
and can use

704
00:26:33,192 --> 00:26:34,995
a variety
of operating systems?

705
00:26:35,028 --> 00:26:37,497
Well, I guess that's a start.

706
00:26:37,530 --> 00:26:41,133
Well... I've undergone
a complex personal evolution

707
00:26:41,167 --> 00:26:43,102
wherein painful confusion
has given way

708
00:26:43,135 --> 00:26:45,538
to what I like to think of
as some degree of wisdom

709
00:26:45,572 --> 00:26:48,808
culminating in my current
zarathustrian sense of self.

710
00:26:48,842 --> 00:26:50,743
- Is that it?
- No.

711
00:26:50,777 --> 00:26:53,145
If that helps you,
it's good, but...

712
00:26:53,178 --> 00:26:55,382
Gypsy, I don't know what--

713
00:26:55,415 --> 00:26:57,717
is it that I often panic
while making sandwiches?

714
00:26:57,750 --> 00:26:58,818
Yeah!
Well...

715
00:26:58,852 --> 00:27:00,887
Well, maybe it's that kind
of thing.

716
00:27:00,920 --> 00:27:02,588
Is it that I smell conspiracy
in everything

717
00:27:02,622 --> 00:27:05,958
and I don't know
what I mean most of the time?

718
00:27:05,992 --> 00:27:10,163
Oh, that's perhaps a small piece
of the puzzle, but I...

719
00:27:10,197 --> 00:27:11,832
Gypsy...

720
00:27:11,865 --> 00:27:12,932
Is it an odor?

721
00:27:12,965 --> 00:27:14,267
No. Well...

722
00:27:14,300 --> 00:27:15,868
No.

723
00:27:15,902 --> 00:27:18,137
Well, maybe it's Tom
I don't get.

724
00:27:18,170 --> 00:27:19,638
Oh, brother.

725
00:27:19,672 --> 00:27:22,908
Like a zippo lighter without
any flint, I can't believe...

726
00:27:25,612 --> 00:27:27,347
[Humming]

727
00:27:27,380 --> 00:27:28,348
Ugh.

728
00:27:28,382 --> 00:27:29,648
- Tom?
- Yo.

729
00:27:29,682 --> 00:27:31,150
I don't get you.

730
00:27:31,183 --> 00:27:32,185
Nobody does.

731
00:27:32,218 --> 00:27:33,620
I'm the wind, baby.

732
00:27:33,653 --> 00:27:35,355
[Humming]

733
00:27:45,865 --> 00:27:47,167
Hmm.
[Humming]

734
00:27:47,200 --> 00:27:48,635
Jeez, aren't there any roads
in this town?

735
00:27:48,668 --> 00:27:50,703
It's a hover bonneville.

736
00:27:50,737 --> 00:27:53,138
I think this is the movie
version of "darkness visible,"

737
00:27:53,172 --> 00:27:54,807
by William styron.

738
00:27:54,840 --> 00:27:56,375
Wait, I want
to come with you.

739
00:27:56,408 --> 00:27:58,210
No, Ruth, you stay here.

740
00:27:58,243 --> 00:28:00,313
Jeez, you're high-maintenance.

741
00:28:00,347 --> 00:28:01,748
♪♪

742
00:28:01,782 --> 00:28:04,850
Well, um, could I have the keys
so I can listen to the radio?

743
00:28:04,883 --> 00:28:07,553
- Gee.
- Yes?

744
00:28:07,587 --> 00:28:09,055
Jim Taylor.

745
00:28:09,088 --> 00:28:10,657
The helicopter pilot.

746
00:28:10,690 --> 00:28:11,690
How did it happen?

747
00:28:11,725 --> 00:28:12,992
We don't know, sir.

748
00:28:13,025 --> 00:28:16,195
We were having contact with him
from the ground car.

749
00:28:16,228 --> 00:28:18,998
Suddenly, he let out
a yell and then went blank.

750
00:28:19,032 --> 00:28:20,367
There was nothing we could do.

751
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:21,935
You'd better take a look
at the body, Carl.

752
00:28:21,968 --> 00:28:24,470
He said "garp," then he said
"good," and then he died.

753
00:28:24,503 --> 00:28:25,772
I've never seen
anything like it.

754
00:28:25,805 --> 00:28:27,840
He's shriveled up like
a dried prune.

755
00:28:27,873 --> 00:28:29,175
Like a drag queen?

756
00:28:29,208 --> 00:28:31,010
Looks like
some kind of a burn.

757
00:28:31,043 --> 00:28:33,513
But it didn't touch his hair
or his clothes.

758
00:28:33,547 --> 00:28:36,148
But he's horribly mutilated.

759
00:28:36,182 --> 00:28:38,050
That's what's
left of him.

760
00:28:38,083 --> 00:28:40,720
They shaved his moustache,
those animals!

761
00:28:40,753 --> 00:28:42,022
There's something else
I want to show you.

762
00:28:46,392 --> 00:28:48,393
Uh, Rick,
could I get a ride home?

763
00:28:48,428 --> 00:28:49,895
What do you make of this,
Carl?

764
00:28:49,928 --> 00:28:52,065
Looks like
a Meadow muffin.

765
00:28:52,098 --> 00:28:54,200
They look like severe burns.

766
00:28:54,233 --> 00:28:55,968
What could have
caused such burns?

767
00:28:56,002 --> 00:28:57,803
Mexican food?

768
00:28:57,837 --> 00:28:59,605
I don't know.

769
00:28:59,638 --> 00:29:02,575
Probably some kids' prank.

770
00:29:02,608 --> 00:29:04,710
Yeah, kids are always
mutilating people.

771
00:29:04,743 --> 00:29:05,878
Take the body
back to the hospital.

772
00:29:05,912 --> 00:29:07,280
Oh, good, now what about
the capsule?

773
00:29:07,313 --> 00:29:08,782
The equipment's
on the way now.

774
00:29:08,815 --> 00:29:11,550
All right, but I want you
to stay here until it arrives.

775
00:29:11,583 --> 00:29:14,053
I don't want anything disturbed
until Dr. Logan has had a chance

776
00:29:14,087 --> 00:29:15,555
to examine it carefully.

777
00:29:15,588 --> 00:29:18,157
All right, Dr. Schreiber.

778
00:29:18,190 --> 00:29:20,092
We'd better get
back to the lab.

779
00:29:20,127 --> 00:29:22,095
There's nothing more
we can do here.

780
00:29:22,128 --> 00:29:23,697
Robert goulet
and Martin milner--

781
00:29:23,730 --> 00:29:25,998
they're cops.

782
00:29:26,032 --> 00:29:27,767
What are we gonna tell Ruth?

783
00:29:27,800 --> 00:29:29,668
What do you mean "we"?

784
00:29:29,702 --> 00:29:31,270
Can we, Carl?

785
00:29:31,303 --> 00:29:32,605
What do you mean?

786
00:29:32,638 --> 00:29:35,040
There's no real proof
that Douglas is dead.

787
00:29:35,073 --> 00:29:37,043
- There's no body.
- No bounce.

788
00:29:37,077 --> 00:29:38,745
Maybe we shouldn't
tell her anything.

789
00:29:38,778 --> 00:29:40,680
Yeah, let's toy
with her for a while.

790
00:29:40,713 --> 00:29:42,548
Nobody hopes for a miracle
more than I, Henry.

791
00:29:42,582 --> 00:29:43,717
The pope does.

792
00:29:43,750 --> 00:29:46,385
There's no sense
grabbing at straws.

793
00:29:46,418 --> 00:29:48,888
Even assuming he was
blown clear of the wreckage

794
00:29:48,922 --> 00:29:50,790
before the doctors
had ran out.

795
00:29:50,823 --> 00:29:52,358
You're deep, Terry.

796
00:29:52,392 --> 00:29:54,460
The wreckage here is enough
to tell the story.

797
00:29:54,493 --> 00:29:56,062
I'm not convinced.

798
00:29:56,095 --> 00:29:59,198
The condition
of that body...

799
00:29:59,232 --> 00:30:00,967
Something's not right.

800
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:03,302
We'll probably get the facts
in the laboratory.

801
00:30:03,335 --> 00:30:04,770
Maybe you're right.

802
00:30:04,803 --> 00:30:06,272
Hungry?

803
00:30:07,873 --> 00:30:11,143
[Scoffs] Couldn't even afford
to hang the chalkboard.

804
00:30:11,177 --> 00:30:12,645
Still mad, Evan?

805
00:30:12,678 --> 00:30:14,413
Let's talk.

806
00:30:14,447 --> 00:30:16,248
I thought you went home.

807
00:30:16,282 --> 00:30:17,717
Too much on my mind.

808
00:30:17,750 --> 00:30:19,752
Uh, you've got a bit
of toilet paper on your shoe.

809
00:30:19,785 --> 00:30:20,787
When did you get back
to the lab?

810
00:30:20,820 --> 00:30:22,055
I never went home either.

811
00:30:22,088 --> 00:30:23,957
I kept thinking
about the body.

812
00:30:23,990 --> 00:30:27,160
Something about the capsule
still haunts me.

813
00:30:27,193 --> 00:30:28,728
But I'm trying
to find an answer.

814
00:30:28,762 --> 00:30:30,062
Try to find a microphone!

815
00:30:30,095 --> 00:30:31,297
Here I am.

816
00:30:31,330 --> 00:30:32,598
Did you come up
with anything?

817
00:30:32,632 --> 00:30:33,600
Take a look.

818
00:30:33,633 --> 00:30:34,800
You know enough
about these things.

819
00:30:34,833 --> 00:30:37,803
- Hmm?
- Hey, that's my wife!

820
00:30:37,837 --> 00:30:39,238
It's a human tissue.

821
00:30:39,272 --> 00:30:40,640
Correct.

822
00:30:40,673 --> 00:30:43,008
But it's different, though.

823
00:30:43,042 --> 00:30:44,843
I've never seen
anything quite like it.

824
00:30:44,877 --> 00:30:46,012
I took it from
Taylor's body.

825
00:30:46,045 --> 00:30:47,347
Huh?

826
00:30:47,380 --> 00:30:49,115
He was literally cooked to death
in a matter of seconds.

827
00:30:49,148 --> 00:30:50,517
- That sounds delicious.
- By what?

828
00:30:50,550 --> 00:30:53,285
Something so charged with
radiated energy,

829
00:30:53,318 --> 00:30:55,153
contact caused
instant death.

830
00:30:55,187 --> 00:30:58,457
You infer the capsule came back
with radioactive energy?

831
00:30:58,490 --> 00:31:00,393
The capsule
didn't kill Taylor.

832
00:31:00,427 --> 00:31:01,527
Here.

833
00:31:01,560 --> 00:31:06,065
Look, could we get a miracle-ear
or something?

834
00:31:06,098 --> 00:31:08,533
Tiny scraps of metal...

835
00:31:08,568 --> 00:31:09,535
Seems normal.

836
00:31:09,568 --> 00:31:11,803
It is--
no radioactivity.

837
00:31:11,837 --> 00:31:13,138
I don't like you.

838
00:31:13,172 --> 00:31:14,773
Then it was something else.

839
00:31:14,807 --> 00:31:17,410
Remember the scorch Mark
on the ground near the body?

840
00:31:17,443 --> 00:31:18,543
Yeah, that was fun.

841
00:31:18,578 --> 00:31:20,178
Whatever killed Taylor stood

842
00:31:20,212 --> 00:31:22,548
while it was placed
right were the body was.

843
00:31:22,582 --> 00:31:24,983
Analysis of the grass
from that spot

844
00:31:25,017 --> 00:31:27,387
showed clearly the same effect
was on Taylor's body.

845
00:31:27,420 --> 00:31:28,855
Henry, I know what
you're driving at but...

846
00:31:28,888 --> 00:31:31,523
- Shut up.
- But it's impossible.

847
00:31:31,557 --> 00:31:33,492
Douglas couldn't
have survived.

848
00:31:33,525 --> 00:31:34,993
Why not?

849
00:31:35,027 --> 00:31:37,197
The nose cone
of the capsule did.

850
00:31:37,230 --> 00:31:39,798
- The chute opened.
- I'm aware of that.

851
00:31:39,832 --> 00:31:40,933
Though we both
know that it opened

852
00:31:40,967 --> 00:31:42,635
while he passed over
our radius.

853
00:31:42,668 --> 00:31:44,103
Passover gradient?

854
00:31:44,137 --> 00:31:46,172
Maybe if I go along with you,
I'll hear something that--

855
00:31:46,205 --> 00:31:47,307
that cycles [indistinct].

856
00:31:47,340 --> 00:31:49,542
That's...
A fragment boo-bad?

857
00:31:49,575 --> 00:31:51,343
And why should it be
radioactive?

858
00:31:51,377 --> 00:31:52,712
Washington's radioactive?

859
00:31:52,745 --> 00:31:54,280
I thought it was lower
than [indistinct].

860
00:31:54,313 --> 00:31:55,782
With a road apple?

861
00:31:55,815 --> 00:31:57,150
I don't have any answers
to those questions yet.

862
00:31:57,183 --> 00:31:58,985
But my brother and I
are working on it.

863
00:31:59,018 --> 00:32:00,853
He's rather
Dr. Bellows-esque.

864
00:32:00,887 --> 00:32:02,488
- Let's face facts.
- Mm-hmm.

865
00:32:02,522 --> 00:32:04,823
One, a capsule comes back.

866
00:32:04,857 --> 00:32:05,958
One cat in a bag?

867
00:32:05,992 --> 00:32:08,360
A man is seared to death
by an unknown force.

868
00:32:08,393 --> 00:32:09,428
- Five.
- Three...

869
00:32:09,462 --> 00:32:10,730
No, three, sir.

870
00:32:10,763 --> 00:32:11,830
We know an excessive amount
of radiation came back

871
00:32:11,863 --> 00:32:13,833
with the capsule,
and four...

872
00:32:13,867 --> 00:32:15,468
- I'm Percy dovetonsils.
- Douglas is missing.

873
00:32:15,502 --> 00:32:18,037
But the radiation
is what bothers me.

874
00:32:18,070 --> 00:32:20,138
Did you tell Dr. Manning
of this?

875
00:32:20,172 --> 00:32:21,807
No, not yet.

876
00:32:21,840 --> 00:32:24,143
And I'd appreciate it
if you wouldn't either.

877
00:32:24,177 --> 00:32:27,380
At least until we have
some more answers.

878
00:32:27,413 --> 00:32:28,915
Do I need a shave?

879
00:32:28,948 --> 00:32:30,917
Well, it's entirely
up to you, Henry.

880
00:32:30,950 --> 00:32:32,785
Take as much
time as you need.

881
00:32:32,818 --> 00:32:34,453
Bacon as much time
as you need?

882
00:32:34,487 --> 00:32:35,855
Let's pray that something else
doesn't happen.

883
00:32:35,888 --> 00:32:36,990
Starting...

884
00:32:37,023 --> 00:32:40,192
Now, go!

885
00:32:40,225 --> 00:32:41,260
Good night.

886
00:32:41,293 --> 00:32:42,362
- Ah.
- Good night.

887
00:32:42,395 --> 00:32:44,063
Hmm, "dear diary, well,
you won't believe

888
00:32:44,097 --> 00:32:46,532
who waltzed
back into my life today..."

889
00:32:46,565 --> 00:32:47,867
You know what?

890
00:32:47,900 --> 00:32:49,035
This is like "the blob."

891
00:32:49,068 --> 00:32:50,102
No, no, "the thing."

892
00:32:50,135 --> 00:32:51,237
No, it's "it."

893
00:32:51,270 --> 00:32:52,705
Or maybe "them."

894
00:32:52,738 --> 00:32:54,673
No, it's "three coins
in the fountain."

895
00:32:54,707 --> 00:32:57,077
Uh, sort of... thing.

896
00:32:57,110 --> 00:32:59,345
So I guess
we're through, then.

897
00:32:59,378 --> 00:33:00,580
We're finally going to meet
the invisible member

898
00:33:00,613 --> 00:33:02,515
- of this project.
- Yes.

899
00:33:02,548 --> 00:33:03,817
Strange feeling,
working three years

900
00:33:03,850 --> 00:33:05,050
with a man you've never met.

901
00:33:05,083 --> 00:33:06,518
He must be talking about
his agent.

902
00:33:06,552 --> 00:33:08,287
[Laughs]

903
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:10,723
Oh.

904
00:33:10,757 --> 00:33:14,693
We will return to George Kennedy
in "airport '75."

905
00:33:18,463 --> 00:33:23,002
Jeez, I hate having to drive
my friends to the airport, god.

906
00:33:27,507 --> 00:33:30,042
Mm, ah, sorry,
I broke the ladder!

907
00:33:30,075 --> 00:33:32,612
[Grunting]

908
00:33:32,645 --> 00:33:36,448
Early in 1965, president Johnson
visited Saigon.

909
00:33:36,482 --> 00:33:38,383
He was greeted
by general westmoreland.

910
00:33:38,417 --> 00:33:40,720
The episode was considered
important enough

911
00:33:40,753 --> 00:33:42,522
for the civilian head
of the project,

912
00:33:42,555 --> 00:33:47,127
Dr. Chris Manning,
to make an emergency visit.

913
00:33:47,160 --> 00:33:48,560
Hi, Chris.

914
00:33:48,593 --> 00:33:50,497
Carl.

915
00:33:50,530 --> 00:33:53,265
It isn't often we have such
a short time between visits.

916
00:33:53,298 --> 00:33:54,300
How are you, Connors?

917
00:33:54,333 --> 00:33:55,300
Fine,
thank you, general.

918
00:33:55,333 --> 00:33:56,535
He's the sky cap,
you dope!

919
00:33:56,568 --> 00:33:58,637
Dr. Chris Manning,
Dr. Schreiber, captain Connors.

920
00:33:58,670 --> 00:33:59,638
- Captain.
- Hi, major.

921
00:33:59,672 --> 00:34:01,107
Chris, colonel.
Colonel, Chris.

922
00:34:01,140 --> 00:34:02,842
I've been looking forward
to meeting you for a long time.

923
00:34:02,875 --> 00:34:04,210
The pleasure's mine, doctor.

924
00:34:04,243 --> 00:34:06,245
I've been impressed
with some of your theories.

925
00:34:06,278 --> 00:34:07,947
I'd like to discuss them
with you one day.

926
00:34:07,980 --> 00:34:09,782
- Over cocktails.
- I'd like that.

927
00:34:09,815 --> 00:34:13,018
I pulled Chris off of vacation
to come down here with me.

928
00:34:13,052 --> 00:34:14,353
And his secretary.

929
00:34:14,387 --> 00:34:15,622
After Logan told me
about the pilot,

930
00:34:15,655 --> 00:34:18,123
I decided it was time
to break security

931
00:34:18,157 --> 00:34:19,492
and bring you two together.

932
00:34:19,525 --> 00:34:22,462
I would like to examine the body
as soon as possible.

933
00:34:22,495 --> 00:34:24,297
We should be finished
with the report by now.

934
00:34:24,330 --> 00:34:25,297
We can go to the hospital...

935
00:34:25,330 --> 00:34:27,100
- His face is gone!
- Fine.

936
00:34:27,133 --> 00:34:28,802
I'll fill you in on the details
on the way over.

937
00:34:28,835 --> 00:34:30,803
- Oh.
- It's a drive-by shooting!

938
00:34:30,837 --> 00:34:31,870
[Imitating gunshots]

939
00:34:31,903 --> 00:34:33,438
So, beef 'n barrel?

940
00:34:33,472 --> 00:34:34,673
That sound--
how about barnaby's?

941
00:34:34,707 --> 00:34:35,875
Barnaby's, then, huh?
Barnaby's is good.

942
00:34:35,908 --> 00:34:36,875
Yeah, barnaby's.

943
00:34:36,908 --> 00:34:37,943
- Yep.
- That'll be good.

944
00:34:37,977 --> 00:34:39,712
Hey, general,
midasize it, huh?

945
00:34:39,745 --> 00:34:42,215
Rattle-rattle, clutter-clutter,
boom-boom-boom...

946
00:34:43,983 --> 00:34:46,352
Weird, it gets quieter
when you close the door.

947
00:34:46,385 --> 00:34:48,087
Hmm.

948
00:34:48,120 --> 00:34:51,823
[Imitating bassy car stereo]

949
00:34:51,857 --> 00:34:53,758
Hey.

950
00:34:53,793 --> 00:34:55,628
- [Gasps]
- Why, general!

951
00:34:55,662 --> 00:34:57,297
♪♪

952
00:34:57,330 --> 00:34:58,965
Oh, they decided to go
to shakey's.

953
00:34:58,998 --> 00:35:00,933
- Ohh.
[Laughter]

954
00:35:05,872 --> 00:35:06,905
Hello, Laverne.

955
00:35:06,938 --> 00:35:08,307
Hello, shirl.

956
00:35:08,340 --> 00:35:09,608
♪♪

957
00:35:09,642 --> 00:35:11,777
It's "the Patty Duke show."

958
00:35:14,247 --> 00:35:17,583
♪ Rock rock rock rock rock
and roll high school... ♪

959
00:35:18,985 --> 00:35:20,787
Oh, there's Lenny Bruce
over there.

960
00:35:20,820 --> 00:35:23,022
Hmm.

961
00:35:23,055 --> 00:35:25,423
Must be dancing
at the slipped disc.

962
00:35:27,493 --> 00:35:31,130
A sullen Jack kerouac pulls out
a "j" in the middle of the room.

963
00:35:32,632 --> 00:35:34,933
Chad, the sensitive teen.

964
00:35:34,967 --> 00:35:37,970
Hey, you put your cigarette out
in my black cow!

965
00:35:38,003 --> 00:35:39,638
Hmm.

966
00:35:39,672 --> 00:35:42,508
So, this is what goes on
in the teacher's lounge, huh?

967
00:35:42,542 --> 00:35:44,310
[Laughter]

968
00:35:44,343 --> 00:35:47,180
I'll have a little of this
balsamic vinegar here.

969
00:35:47,213 --> 00:35:49,382
Yeah.
Mm, mm.

970
00:35:49,415 --> 00:35:52,050
Gah! Not good, really.

971
00:35:52,083 --> 00:35:53,987
[Clears throat
and coughs]

972
00:35:54,020 --> 00:35:56,355
I'm pretty sure this is where
the movie really takes off.

973
00:35:56,388 --> 00:35:57,457
This is the "a-go go" part.

974
00:35:57,490 --> 00:36:00,125
Whoa, he's got
an action torso there.

975
00:36:00,158 --> 00:36:02,093
Ha-ha, I'm dancing!

976
00:36:02,127 --> 00:36:05,030
What is this,
"the Lloyd thaxton show"?

977
00:36:05,063 --> 00:36:07,367
Kinda.

978
00:36:07,400 --> 00:36:08,667
Not him, honey.

979
00:36:08,700 --> 00:36:10,737
He's got action hips.

980
00:36:10,770 --> 00:36:13,538
The Alpha male
dominates the herd.

981
00:36:13,572 --> 00:36:15,608
Come on back here
and fight like a girl!

982
00:36:15,642 --> 00:36:17,108
Come on!

983
00:36:17,142 --> 00:36:19,345
Aww.

984
00:36:19,378 --> 00:36:21,047
Hmm? Hmm?

985
00:36:21,080 --> 00:36:22,180
♪♪

986
00:36:22,215 --> 00:36:24,617
That's a strange car alarm.

987
00:36:24,650 --> 00:36:26,552
♪♪

988
00:36:26,585 --> 00:36:29,088
Django rinehart
tunes up his guitar.

989
00:36:29,122 --> 00:36:33,258
♪ Here in my car,
I feel safest of all ♪

990
00:36:33,292 --> 00:36:35,260
The thin blue line.

991
00:36:38,030 --> 00:36:38,998
♪♪

992
00:36:39,032 --> 00:36:43,002
[All]
♪ eee-oh ♪

993
00:36:45,638 --> 00:36:50,208
Huh, well, I guess introductions
are in order, heh.

994
00:36:50,242 --> 00:36:53,145
What changes the delicate
interlocking of fates

995
00:36:53,178 --> 00:36:55,715
that determines
life or death?

996
00:36:55,748 --> 00:36:57,483
A series of ifs.

997
00:36:57,517 --> 00:36:58,683
Ands or buts.

998
00:36:58,717 --> 00:37:00,620
If the girl had danced
with her boyfriend

999
00:37:00,653 --> 00:37:02,187
instead of the other boy,

1000
00:37:02,220 --> 00:37:04,290
and they
had stayed later,

1001
00:37:04,323 --> 00:37:08,360
if the two of them hadn't
parked to kiss and make up...

1002
00:37:08,393 --> 00:37:10,228
But that
is not what happened,

1003
00:37:10,262 --> 00:37:14,433
and fate and history
never deal in "ifs."

1004
00:37:14,467 --> 00:37:17,470
I'm hearing your
internal monologue, honey.

1005
00:37:20,707 --> 00:37:22,107
[Dog barking]

1006
00:37:22,140 --> 00:37:23,375
He made her bark!

1007
00:37:23,408 --> 00:37:25,043
Ooh.

1008
00:37:33,152 --> 00:37:35,688
I have to let that dog
out of the trunk.

1009
00:37:35,722 --> 00:37:37,188
It's creepy the clown.

1010
00:37:37,222 --> 00:37:39,425
[Screaming]

1011
00:37:39,458 --> 00:37:40,660
♪♪

1012
00:37:40,693 --> 00:37:43,795
♪ eee-oh ♪

1013
00:37:43,828 --> 00:37:46,998
Thank goodness my allstate man
was on the scene.

1014
00:37:47,032 --> 00:37:51,170
Are we awfully fast,
or was that a jump cut?

1015
00:37:51,203 --> 00:37:54,107
He's mangled in another horrible
way I've never seen before.

1016
00:37:54,140 --> 00:37:56,475
- That guy's creative.
- Good god.

1017
00:37:56,508 --> 00:37:58,643
- Jump back, kiss myself.
- What happened to him?

1018
00:37:58,677 --> 00:38:00,045
From the looks of him,
general,

1019
00:38:00,078 --> 00:38:01,113
I'd say the same thing
that happened

1020
00:38:01,147 --> 00:38:02,515
to the helicopter pilot.

1021
00:38:02,548 --> 00:38:04,050
Could you turn down
your reverb, Chris?

1022
00:38:04,083 --> 00:38:06,485
- Who found him, captain?
- One of the search party.

1023
00:38:06,518 --> 00:38:08,053
Did you see anything?

1024
00:38:08,087 --> 00:38:09,888
No, doctor,
not a thing.

1025
00:38:09,922 --> 00:38:12,625
Ah, you two.

1026
00:38:12,658 --> 00:38:13,792
Hey, nice car.

1027
00:38:13,825 --> 00:38:15,393
Nobody wants it,
I'll take it.

1028
00:38:15,427 --> 00:38:18,663
Wow, look at all
the butane cans!

