00:47:11,997 --> 00:47:12,965
We'll join you there.

1280
00:47:12,998 --> 00:47:14,132
All right, doctor.

1281
00:47:14,165 --> 00:47:15,133
I'll go with you.

1282
00:47:15,167 --> 00:47:16,402
They can bring my car later.

1283
00:47:16,435 --> 00:47:18,903
Well, thank you, pinky.

1284
00:47:18,937 --> 00:47:21,607
Well, we'll just field strip him
and get out of here.

1285
00:47:21,640 --> 00:47:23,575
Do you have any idea of where
Logan was going this morning?

1286
00:47:23,608 --> 00:47:25,343
Well, when we got out
of bed there's a-- oh!

1287
00:47:25,377 --> 00:47:27,678
He said he was going into town
to pick up some personal things.

1288
00:47:27,712 --> 00:47:29,213
He was on a wifely errand.

1289
00:47:29,247 --> 00:47:30,982
I don't know of any special
plans he had.

1290
00:47:31,015 --> 00:47:33,618
Mentioned something
about "the horror."

1291
00:47:33,652 --> 00:47:36,422
If we can find out what brought
him to this particular place,

1292
00:47:36,455 --> 00:47:37,622
we might have
something to go on.

1293
00:47:37,655 --> 00:47:38,690
Good idea.

1294
00:47:38,723 --> 00:47:41,092
You know, we work good
together, Chris.

1295
00:47:41,125 --> 00:47:43,095
I'll just wait here then.

1296
00:47:43,128 --> 00:47:44,997
Rot, decompose.

1297
00:47:45,030 --> 00:47:46,565
Rot, decompose.

1298
00:47:46,598 --> 00:47:47,632
Decompose.

1299
00:47:47,665 --> 00:47:49,267
Ah, poor sap.

1300
00:47:50,635 --> 00:47:53,172
Sky king!

1301
00:47:53,205 --> 00:47:55,840
It's Mathias rust.

1302
00:47:55,873 --> 00:47:58,343
Tonight on "wings,"
the beechcraft bonanza

1303
00:47:58,377 --> 00:48:01,880
with its revolutionary
"v" tail.

1304
00:48:01,913 --> 00:48:03,482
[Gasps]

1305
00:48:03,515 --> 00:48:07,685
[Coughing and sputtering]

1306
00:48:07,718 --> 00:48:09,922
Thank you, thing.

1307
00:48:09,955 --> 00:48:11,690
Hey, it's sununu,
going to get a haircut.

1308
00:48:11,723 --> 00:48:13,625
[Laughing]

1309
00:48:13,658 --> 00:48:16,027
'Cause he spent a lot of money
on planes.

1310
00:48:16,060 --> 00:48:17,428
Hot dog burp.

1311
00:48:17,462 --> 00:48:19,665
As far as the tragedies.

1312
00:48:19,698 --> 00:48:20,965
What do you mean?

1313
00:48:20,998 --> 00:48:23,268
Well, for the last eight weeks,
nothing has happened.

1314
00:48:23,302 --> 00:48:25,503
This is like
a totally other movie.

1315
00:48:25,537 --> 00:48:27,038
Whatever came back in that
capsule has disappeared.

1316
00:48:27,072 --> 00:48:28,973
You speak in riddles,
sahib.

1317
00:48:29,007 --> 00:48:31,477
And I thought this
was going to be a lockup.

1318
00:48:31,510 --> 00:48:32,777
I thought it
was just legwork

1319
00:48:32,810 --> 00:48:34,278
when Manning turned it over
to me.

1320
00:48:34,312 --> 00:48:36,013
- Legwork?
- Who are these guys?!

1321
00:48:36,047 --> 00:48:37,548
You know, detailed stuff.
Trivia.

1322
00:48:37,582 --> 00:48:39,652
There's nothing trivial
about three deaths.

1323
00:48:39,685 --> 00:48:43,622
That's for sure, and now I can
understand Chris's concern.

1324
00:48:43,655 --> 00:48:44,957
So let's get with it.

1325
00:48:44,990 --> 00:48:45,957
Well, where
do we start?

1326
00:48:45,990 --> 00:48:47,025
He looks like babe Ruth.

1327
00:48:47,058 --> 00:48:48,293
I'd like
to see Dr. Kramer.

1328
00:48:48,327 --> 00:48:50,295
Well, she's probably
in her lab.

1329
00:48:50,328 --> 00:48:51,763
Does she work alone?

1330
00:48:51,797 --> 00:48:53,932
Well, she started it
with Dr. Logan,

1331
00:48:53,965 --> 00:48:56,233
but now she works
with Conrad, his brother.

1332
00:48:56,267 --> 00:48:57,268
Well, let's go.

1333
00:48:57,302 --> 00:48:58,470
I'm glad
we had this talk.

1334
00:48:58,503 --> 00:49:00,172
I feel much closer
to you now.

1335
00:49:00,205 --> 00:49:01,773
Well, he's impotent.

1336
00:49:03,775 --> 00:49:08,280
And he's impotent, good.

1337
00:49:08,313 --> 00:49:10,682
I'm Chad,
from parke-Davis.

1338
00:49:10,715 --> 00:49:12,483
Dr. Brent,
Dr. Kramer.

1339
00:49:12,517 --> 00:49:13,852
Dr. Moe?

1340
00:49:13,885 --> 00:49:15,020
I'm glad to know you.

1341
00:49:15,053 --> 00:49:16,622
I filled Dr. Brent in
on the details,

1342
00:49:16,655 --> 00:49:18,523
but, well, let's get
to the larger issue.

1343
00:49:18,557 --> 00:49:19,490
Me.

1344
00:49:19,523 --> 00:49:21,058
Floor is yours, doctor.

1345
00:49:21,092 --> 00:49:22,827
Did you run a blood count
on Taylor?

1346
00:49:22,860 --> 00:49:23,828
Yes, we did.

1347
00:49:23,862 --> 00:49:25,263
Actually, there
was no blood.

1348
00:49:25,297 --> 00:49:26,598
It had all turned to powder,

1349
00:49:26,632 --> 00:49:28,267
and we think that accounts
for the shriveled effect.

1350
00:49:28,300 --> 00:49:29,702
Like shrinky dinks.

1351
00:49:29,735 --> 00:49:31,770
Well, how long was Taylor dead
before his body was found?

1352
00:49:31,803 --> 00:49:34,372
- It was just a matter of...
- Um, doctor, i--

1353
00:49:34,405 --> 00:49:36,140
doctor, if you could
look at this slide.

1354
00:49:36,173 --> 00:49:37,542
We think he was on his way
back to the copter...

1355
00:49:37,575 --> 00:49:40,078
Doctor, that full
polio vaccine, Dr. Salk?

1356
00:49:40,112 --> 00:49:41,813
Um, doctor, I think
I found it.

1357
00:49:41,847 --> 00:49:43,015
... it came back
to the capsule.

1358
00:49:43,048 --> 00:49:45,150
Doctor, if you just--
ohh.

1359
00:49:45,183 --> 00:49:46,752
It's true.

1360
00:49:46,785 --> 00:49:49,955
We have a radioactive
something or other.

1361
00:49:49,988 --> 00:49:52,023
- Thingy.
- Ten-foot tall, 400 pounds.

1362
00:49:52,057 --> 00:49:53,225
Well, that's a keeper.

1363
00:49:53,258 --> 00:49:54,558
Connors, if we sent
an astronaut up,

1364
00:49:54,592 --> 00:49:55,793
we got him back.

1365
00:49:55,827 --> 00:49:57,295
Dr. Salk, please, it's--

1366
00:49:57,328 --> 00:50:00,032
it's definitely
the polio vaccine.

1367
00:50:00,065 --> 00:50:02,467
Oh, forget it,
I'll take credit for it.

1368
00:50:02,500 --> 00:50:03,602
It's impossible.

1369
00:50:03,635 --> 00:50:05,237
With the perfect two-man
capsule, easy control...

1370
00:50:05,270 --> 00:50:07,505
Ooh, what a gesture!

1371
00:50:07,538 --> 00:50:08,773
Well, unless
I missed my guess,

1372
00:50:08,807 --> 00:50:09,942
Douglas is back.

1373
00:50:09,975 --> 00:50:11,042
A monster?

1374
00:50:11,075 --> 00:50:12,277
I don't know.

1375
00:50:12,310 --> 00:50:14,112
Apathetic scientists.

1376
00:50:14,145 --> 00:50:16,382
What about
this antedium 51

1377
00:50:16,415 --> 00:50:18,250
he was taking, the radiation
repellent?

1378
00:50:18,283 --> 00:50:21,420
Oh, yes, we actually gave him
lots more of these injections

1379
00:50:21,453 --> 00:50:23,655
so that he could withstand
all kinds of rays.

1380
00:50:23,688 --> 00:50:25,790
We've been working
on big gobs of neat stuff.

1381
00:50:25,823 --> 00:50:28,260
Well, did Dr. Logan
keep a log?

1382
00:50:28,293 --> 00:50:29,260
Certainly.

1383
00:50:29,293 --> 00:50:30,395
May I see it?

1384
00:50:30,428 --> 00:50:31,397
Yes.

1385
00:50:31,430 --> 00:50:32,763
Fine, jeez.

1386
00:50:32,797 --> 00:50:35,700
Weird, weird, weird.

1387
00:50:35,733 --> 00:50:38,537
Okay, let's see--
there's my journal,

1388
00:50:38,570 --> 00:50:40,005
this is my coin
collection book,

1389
00:50:40,038 --> 00:50:42,073
and then my book
of s&h green stamps.

1390
00:50:42,107 --> 00:50:44,810
This is Dr. Logan's log.
You'll find it very correct.

1391
00:50:44,843 --> 00:50:47,278
Well, let me see here.
Ahem!

1392
00:50:47,312 --> 00:50:50,215
Rumple tweezer, the good furry
lip, under the dum-dum tree?

1393
00:50:50,248 --> 00:50:52,683
What? Doh!

1394
00:50:52,717 --> 00:50:55,453
Regular Evelyn wood,
isn't he?

1395
00:50:55,487 --> 00:50:57,122
Do you keep a log?

1396
00:50:57,155 --> 00:50:58,357
Sure.

1397
00:50:58,390 --> 00:50:59,992
May I see it, please?

1398
00:51:00,025 --> 00:51:01,392
Yes, I'll get it.

1399
00:51:01,427 --> 00:51:04,062
Sure, I'll just
shelve my cancer research.

1400
00:51:12,537 --> 00:51:14,572
[Imitating rocky the squirrel]
"Bullwinkle is a"--

1401
00:51:14,605 --> 00:51:16,107
[imitating bullwinkle]
Not that lesson.

1402
00:51:16,140 --> 00:51:17,708
This lesson.

1403
00:51:20,012 --> 00:51:21,780
Ew, mayonnaise!

1404
00:51:21,813 --> 00:51:23,482
Sure is thorough.

1405
00:51:23,515 --> 00:51:24,482
That's why he's here.

1406
00:51:24,515 --> 00:51:27,285
He's thorough, yet gentle.

1407
00:51:27,318 --> 00:51:29,855
Why did you change
from antedium 50 to 51

1408
00:51:29,888 --> 00:51:30,888
six months ago?

1409
00:51:30,922 --> 00:51:32,023
Speaking of tedium...

1410
00:51:32,057 --> 00:51:33,258
Dr. Logan thought it would be
more effective.

1411
00:51:33,292 --> 00:51:35,027
The 50 was very effective.

1412
00:51:35,060 --> 00:51:38,095
It was tested on animals...
A two-year period,

1413
00:51:38,130 --> 00:51:39,530
the other for only six months.

1414
00:51:39,563 --> 00:51:40,598
I didn't know that.

1415
00:51:40,632 --> 00:51:41,800
Why wasn't I told?

1416
00:51:41,833 --> 00:51:44,837
Listen, I'm feeling
a little picked on here.

1417
00:51:44,870 --> 00:51:46,872
Dr. Logan was so sure
it would be all right.

1418
00:51:46,905 --> 00:51:48,340
Shame-based science.

1419
00:51:48,373 --> 00:51:49,507
Oh, another thing, doctor.

1420
00:51:49,540 --> 00:51:51,910
Dr. Logan states
this final injection

1421
00:51:51,943 --> 00:51:54,312
with Douglas is 100 ccs.

1422
00:51:54,345 --> 00:51:55,713
According to your log,

1423
00:51:55,747 --> 00:51:58,283
as compared to his,
yours says 200 ccs.

1424
00:51:58,317 --> 00:52:00,685
We keep two sets of books.
[Chuckles]

1425
00:52:00,718 --> 00:52:02,553
Who's correct, doctor?

1426
00:52:02,587 --> 00:52:04,957
I am only an assistant
in this laboratory.

1427
00:52:04,990 --> 00:52:07,092
I simply work here,
and I take my orders!

1428
00:52:07,125 --> 00:52:08,560
Dr. Brent didn't mean that.

1429
00:52:08,593 --> 00:52:09,760
Well, yes, I did,
actually.

1430
00:52:09,793 --> 00:52:11,797
Security must be upheld.

1431
00:52:11,830 --> 00:52:14,833
Where's Dr. Logan?

1432
00:52:14,867 --> 00:52:16,400
He's either in the commissary,

1433
00:52:16,433 --> 00:52:19,170
or perhaps he's back
in the other laboratory.

1434
00:52:19,203 --> 00:52:20,738
Well, let's go see him.

1435
00:52:22,440 --> 00:52:24,808
Perhaps we can
have drinks sometime, hmm?

1436
00:52:24,843 --> 00:52:26,010
Thank you.

1437
00:52:26,043 --> 00:52:28,547
Later, over lunch
with Wally Shawn.

1438
00:52:28,580 --> 00:52:30,048
I deal in facts.

1439
00:52:30,082 --> 00:52:32,450
I cross-checked
your logs with Dr. Kramer's.

1440
00:52:32,483 --> 00:52:33,918
That's proper.

1441
00:52:33,952 --> 00:52:35,420
You're head of the project.

1442
00:52:35,453 --> 00:52:37,222
Well, why wasn't I informed
about the switch

1443
00:52:37,255 --> 00:52:38,890
from antedium 50 to 51?

1444
00:52:38,923 --> 00:52:40,058
'Cause we hate you.

1445
00:52:40,092 --> 00:52:41,425
They're almost identical.

1446
00:52:41,458 --> 00:52:42,993
Well, then why not
call it 50-a?

1447
00:52:43,027 --> 00:52:44,528
I said almost identical.

1448
00:52:44,562 --> 00:52:46,898
Or heinz 57.

1449
00:52:46,932 --> 00:52:51,035
Antedium 51 can stand one-half
times more radiation.

1450
00:52:51,068 --> 00:52:53,305
You mean the subject
can stand more?

1451
00:52:53,338 --> 00:52:54,538
Precisely.

1452
00:52:54,572 --> 00:52:56,107
Hey, this ain't
rocket science, you know.

1453
00:52:56,140 --> 00:52:57,775
How did the animal experiments
work out?

1454
00:52:57,808 --> 00:52:58,943
- Perfectly.
- Fantastic.

1455
00:52:58,977 --> 00:53:00,078
For six months.

1456
00:53:00,112 --> 00:53:02,980
Well, what would happen
with, uh, overdose?

1457
00:53:03,013 --> 00:53:05,650
An overdose of anything,

1458
00:53:05,683 --> 00:53:08,220
candy with a child,
has a bad effect,

1459
00:53:08,253 --> 00:53:10,422
but is correctable.

1460
00:53:10,455 --> 00:53:12,257
Where's our pizza?

1461
00:53:12,290 --> 00:53:13,925
I just wanna go,
let's just go.

1462
00:53:13,958 --> 00:53:17,762
... with 200 ccs instead
of their customary 100 ccs?

1463
00:53:17,795 --> 00:53:19,130
Or was that
your brother's order?

1464
00:53:19,163 --> 00:53:20,898
- No.
- It was a joke!

1465
00:53:20,932 --> 00:53:24,602
Dr. Kramer is very efficient
and very observant.

1466
00:53:24,635 --> 00:53:27,172
I did give him an extra 100 ccs
for added protection.

1467
00:53:27,205 --> 00:53:28,973
That was my reason.

1468
00:53:29,007 --> 00:53:30,642
Did you ever overdose
an animal?

1469
00:53:30,675 --> 00:53:32,577
Well, I smoked
too much rabbit once.

1470
00:53:32,610 --> 00:53:36,147
It absorbed great quantities
of radiation.

1471
00:53:36,180 --> 00:53:37,515
The results, please.

1472
00:53:37,548 --> 00:53:39,517
The winner
in Los Angeles is...

1473
00:53:39,550 --> 00:53:41,485
... the other pigs.

1474
00:53:41,518 --> 00:53:45,590
So every one of them
it touched innocently.

1475
00:53:45,623 --> 00:53:47,025
Is that all?

1476
00:53:47,058 --> 00:53:48,025
No.

1477
00:53:48,058 --> 00:53:49,727
Whoever you are.

1478
00:53:49,760 --> 00:53:53,063
It grew twice
its normal size.

1479
00:53:53,097 --> 00:53:54,465
My brother developed
an antidote.

1480
00:53:54,498 --> 00:53:56,768
My brother's antidote can
beat your brother's antidote.

1481
00:53:56,802 --> 00:53:58,437
The animal died.

1482
00:53:58,470 --> 00:54:00,138
Of what, doctor?

1483
00:54:00,172 --> 00:54:01,940
The heart couldn't stand
the violent transition

1484
00:54:01,973 --> 00:54:03,240
back to normal.

1485
00:54:03,275 --> 00:54:04,275
And Douglas?

1486
00:54:04,308 --> 00:54:05,543
He's alive out there.

1487
00:54:05,577 --> 00:54:07,712
So another leinie?

1488
00:54:09,813 --> 00:54:10,915
Meanwhile, at lenscrafters...

1489
00:54:10,948 --> 00:54:12,617
Yeah, bad movies
in about an hour, right?

1490
00:54:12,650 --> 00:54:14,218
Working late, aren't you?

1491
00:54:14,252 --> 00:54:15,620
Don't you start on me,
baldy.

1492
00:54:15,653 --> 00:54:18,155
Oh, I just had
to finish this up.

1493
00:54:18,188 --> 00:54:22,293
I'm sorry, Mr. Gower,
please don't hit my sore ear!

1494
00:54:22,327 --> 00:54:24,928
Jeez, you break a beaker
over there or something?

1495
00:54:24,963 --> 00:54:27,398
Phew!

1496
00:54:27,432 --> 00:54:28,900
Isn't this fun
working on our own projects,

1497
00:54:28,933 --> 00:54:30,035
yet working together?

1498
00:54:30,068 --> 00:54:31,703
What do you think
of that Dr. Brent?

1499
00:54:31,737 --> 00:54:33,203
Ah, he's a pig.
Why?

1500
00:54:33,237 --> 00:54:35,973
He's a good man.

1501
00:54:36,007 --> 00:54:38,275
Why?

1502
00:54:38,310 --> 00:54:39,910
I don't know.

1503
00:54:39,943 --> 00:54:44,182
He really sort of scares me
with his exactness.

1504
00:54:44,215 --> 00:54:47,385
Makes you awfully aware
of your responsibility.

1505
00:54:47,418 --> 00:54:48,653
The hell?

1506
00:54:48,687 --> 00:54:49,988
He's got a job
to do.

1507
00:54:50,022 --> 00:54:51,622
I wouldn't want it.

1508
00:54:51,657 --> 00:54:53,358
Got any chlorofluorocarbons
over there?

1509
00:54:53,392 --> 00:54:55,027
Feels kind of
strange, though.

1510
00:54:55,060 --> 00:54:57,128
I mean, it's been so quiet
the past couple of weeks.

1511
00:54:57,162 --> 00:54:58,730
They're making
imposter fragrances.

1512
00:54:58,763 --> 00:54:59,730
Hmm.

1513
00:54:59,763 --> 00:55:01,498
Hope it stays that way.

1514
00:55:01,532 --> 00:55:03,868
Yeah.

1515
00:55:03,902 --> 00:55:06,805
No deaths,
no monster reports.

1516
00:55:06,838 --> 00:55:09,340
- [Sighs]
- Hmm.

1517
00:55:09,373 --> 00:55:11,810
I'm sorry,
you say something?

1518
00:55:11,843 --> 00:55:13,177
Oh.

1519
00:55:13,210 --> 00:55:15,013
I see you've made another batch
of antidote.

1520
00:55:15,047 --> 00:55:16,380
[Alarm rings]
[All] Ahh!

1521
00:55:16,413 --> 00:55:18,983
Pencils down.

1522
00:55:19,017 --> 00:55:24,522
It was your brother's
last order.

1523
00:55:24,555 --> 00:55:26,858
So, it was a good "Martin"
last night.

1524
00:55:26,892 --> 00:55:28,793
"What up?"
I love that part.

1525
00:55:28,827 --> 00:55:30,162
[Sniffing]

1526
00:55:30,195 --> 00:55:33,365
Good god, did you launch
a sulfur bomb back there, huh?

1527
00:55:33,398 --> 00:55:34,765
Whuh!

1528
00:55:34,798 --> 00:55:35,900
Hey, I figured it out!

1529
00:55:35,933 --> 00:55:37,702
He's got his script
taped to the floor.

1530
00:55:37,735 --> 00:55:38,837
[Sighs]
Well...

1531
00:55:38,870 --> 00:55:40,272
Let's play with phosphorus.

1532
00:55:40,305 --> 00:55:41,605
That does it.

1533
00:55:43,273 --> 00:55:44,608
Going home?

1534
00:55:44,642 --> 00:55:46,177
Look, I'm working late.

1535
00:55:46,210 --> 00:55:47,478
Aren't you?

1536
00:55:47,512 --> 00:55:49,180
Soon as I finish cleaning up
my desk.

1537
00:55:49,213 --> 00:55:52,517
This is what happens when you
get trapped in an ionesco play.

1538
00:55:52,550 --> 00:55:55,220
Mmm.
[Chuckles]

1539
00:55:55,253 --> 00:55:56,855
He's not mad at her anymore.

1540
00:55:56,888 --> 00:55:59,623
Yeah, looks like the experiment
was a success.

1541
00:55:59,657 --> 00:56:00,925
[Lecherous laugh]

1542
00:56:00,958 --> 00:56:03,762
I don't get it--
why is she changing lab coats?

1543
00:56:03,795 --> 00:56:05,363
She's got a night shift
at fermilab.

1544
00:56:05,397 --> 00:56:07,298
- Oh.
- Hmm?

1545
00:56:07,332 --> 00:56:08,433
Hmm?

1546
00:56:08,467 --> 00:56:09,600
Good night, doctor.

1547
00:56:12,070 --> 00:56:14,405
Oh, damn,
she loosened my script.

1548
00:56:14,438 --> 00:56:18,042
Hey! I think you got page 25
on your shoe!

1549
00:56:18,075 --> 00:56:20,545
Ahh... heh heh.

1550
00:56:20,578 --> 00:56:21,747
I'm gonna snoop
in her sock drawer.

1551
00:56:21,780 --> 00:56:23,247
[Chuckling]

1552
00:56:23,280 --> 00:56:24,748
Ah.

1553
00:56:27,818 --> 00:56:31,122
[Snoring loudly]

1554
00:56:36,627 --> 00:56:39,330
Dr. Logan did know
where the giant was.

1555
00:56:39,363 --> 00:56:42,133
In a storeroom in that
very building.

1556
00:56:42,167 --> 00:56:45,237
Logan had learned that
massive doses of the antidote

1557
00:56:45,270 --> 00:56:47,338
brought about an almost
human appearance.

1558
00:56:47,372 --> 00:56:48,907
I did not, quit lying!

1559
00:56:48,940 --> 00:56:50,742
... such unpredictable
side effects

1560
00:56:50,775 --> 00:56:54,778
that enough tranquilizer
to subdue ten ordinary men

1561
00:56:54,812 --> 00:56:56,013
had to be given each day.

1562
00:56:56,047 --> 00:56:57,248
On an empty stomach.

1563
00:56:57,282 --> 00:56:59,683
This day,
it was late.

1564
00:56:59,717 --> 00:57:02,020
Logan knew that each
passing minute

1565
00:57:02,053 --> 00:57:04,222
might mean
a return to violence.

1566
00:57:04,255 --> 00:57:05,857
♪ Return to violence ♪

1567
00:57:05,890 --> 00:57:07,625
As it turned out, he was
too late.

1568
00:57:07,658 --> 00:57:08,893
We was too late!

1569
00:57:08,927 --> 00:57:10,128
Like his brother,
the scientist

1570
00:57:10,162 --> 00:57:12,763
had an intuitive knowledge
of the situation,

1571
00:57:12,797 --> 00:57:16,600
plus an extraordinarily
bad sense of timing.

1572
00:57:16,633 --> 00:57:19,603
I feel like he laughs when bad
things happen to his friends.

1573
00:57:19,637 --> 00:57:21,372
Oh, that can
be embarrassing.

1574
00:57:21,405 --> 00:57:24,375
Look, the musical stairs
from the science museum.

1575
00:57:24,408 --> 00:57:27,578
♪♪

1576
00:57:27,612 --> 00:57:31,282
Wow, he's sneaking past
Robert fripp's room.

1577
00:57:31,315 --> 00:57:33,885
♪♪

1578
00:57:33,918 --> 00:57:35,320
[Hacks and spits]

1579
00:57:35,353 --> 00:57:36,520
Hit it!

1580
00:57:36,553 --> 00:57:38,757
♪♪

1581
00:57:38,790 --> 00:57:42,527
Hey, would somebody get the cat
off the Fender rhodes, please?

1582
00:57:42,560 --> 00:57:44,562
From there,
he looks like skitch Henderson.

1583
00:57:46,832 --> 00:57:50,368
♪ Ping ping ping ♪

1584
00:57:54,738 --> 00:57:55,873
[Whistles]

1585
00:57:55,907 --> 00:57:58,643
Oh, we don't need
to see this shot.

1586
00:57:58,677 --> 00:58:02,147
Agh.

1587
00:58:02,180 --> 00:58:04,615
♪♪ [Synthesizer music]

1588
00:58:04,648 --> 00:58:06,083
[All humming
"fly like an eagle"]

1589
00:58:06,117 --> 00:58:08,418
♪ Time keeps on slippin',
slippin...' ♪

1590
00:58:08,452 --> 00:58:10,622
♪ doo doo doo doo ♪

1591
00:58:14,158 --> 00:58:16,093
Ah, well, I guess
I didn't have to go.

1592
00:58:16,127 --> 00:58:18,462
[Electronic droning]

1593
00:58:18,495 --> 00:58:22,067
♪ Oh, what a lucky man
he was ♪

1594
00:58:22,100 --> 00:58:25,068
[All humming out-of-key]

1595
00:58:25,102 --> 00:58:27,205
I love hanging out
after school.

1596
00:58:27,238 --> 00:58:28,405
Nobody around.

1597
00:58:28,438 --> 00:58:29,607
Can go in the girls' room.

1598
00:58:29,640 --> 00:58:31,508
Heh heh.

1599
00:58:31,542 --> 00:58:33,745
Everything used
to make the antidote

1600
00:58:33,778 --> 00:58:36,147
was in wreckage by the time
Logan returned.

1601
00:58:36,180 --> 00:58:37,248
That's wreckage?

1602
00:58:37,282 --> 00:58:39,250
Any chance that might
have existed,

1603
00:58:39,283 --> 00:58:42,187
any hope that sufficient
antidote might be compounded

1604
00:58:42,220 --> 00:58:44,155
to have a lasting effect,

1605
00:58:44,188 --> 00:58:47,725
all this vanished with one sweep
of the monster's arm.

1606
00:58:47,758 --> 00:58:49,793
Or any hope that
the film would end soon.

1607
00:58:49,827 --> 00:58:51,728
Oh, man, he got
my "compuserve" magazine

1608
00:58:51,762 --> 00:58:54,265
and "computer gamer weekly"!

1609
00:58:54,298 --> 00:58:56,767
It might have been nice
to show that scene

1610
00:58:56,800 --> 00:58:59,037
with the monster,
but use your imagination.

1611
00:58:59,070 --> 00:59:01,205
It was true horror.

1612
00:59:01,238 --> 00:59:02,773
Oh, man,
I need a smoke.

1613
00:59:02,807 --> 00:59:04,642
I'm dying for a cigarette.

1614
00:59:04,675 --> 00:59:06,377
I really need a smoke.
Oh--

1615
00:59:10,882 --> 00:59:11,848
hello?

1616
00:59:11,882 --> 00:59:13,383
If you'd like
to make a call...

1617
00:59:13,417 --> 00:59:14,852
Get to laboratory d.

1618
00:59:14,885 --> 00:59:16,187
- If you'd like to make a...
- Hurry.

1619
00:59:16,220 --> 00:59:17,422
Are you listen--
I said, if you'd like to make--

1620
00:59:17,455 --> 00:59:18,923
- this is Dr. Logan.
- Listen to me.

1621
00:59:18,957 --> 00:59:19,990
- If you'd like to make a--
- is Dr. Brent there?

1622
00:59:20,023 --> 00:59:23,193
- Ah, for-- can it.
- Put him on.

1623
00:59:23,227 --> 00:59:24,728
Oh, great, I'm dead.

1624
00:59:24,762 --> 00:59:25,797
It's the end of the world.

1625
00:59:25,830 --> 00:59:27,032
- I'm dead.
- Dr. Brent.

1626
00:59:27,065 --> 00:59:29,100
I'll be right over.

1627
00:59:29,133 --> 00:59:31,435
Yes... it's urgent.

1628
00:59:31,468 --> 00:59:33,503
The call is coming
from within the laboratory!

1629
00:59:33,537 --> 00:59:34,538
Great, I'm dead.

1630
00:59:34,572 --> 00:59:36,473
I'm a dead man--
a dead man and walking

1631
00:59:36,507 --> 00:59:37,875
and talking in my clothes.

1632
00:59:37,908 --> 00:59:40,077
That's how dead I am--
I'm dead.

1633
00:59:40,110 --> 00:59:41,412
- I'm dead.
- What a day.

1634
00:59:41,445 --> 00:59:44,848
I invented gaines-burgers,
and I didn't even mean to!

1635
00:59:44,882 --> 00:59:47,285
Ah, here to borrow
more test tubes?

1636
00:59:47,318 --> 00:59:49,420
Can't keep your own stock up?

1637
00:59:49,453 --> 00:59:51,388
What do you mean
was here?

1638
00:59:51,422 --> 00:59:53,858
I hid him in my radiation lab
for weeks.

1639
00:59:53,892 --> 00:59:56,127
I've been giving him
an injection of antidote.

1640
00:59:56,160 --> 00:59:57,828
- That was dumb, jer.
- Mm-hmm.

1641
00:59:57,862 --> 01:00:00,130
Why weren't we informed?

1642
01:00:00,163 --> 01:00:01,865
Blame it on my state
of shock or--

1643
01:00:01,898 --> 01:00:04,535
blame it on the bossa nova.

1644
01:00:04,568 --> 01:00:06,537
I could only think
to help him first.

1645
01:00:06,570 --> 01:00:07,972
Kiss that nobel good-bye.

1646
01:00:08,005 --> 01:00:09,107
Did the antidote
do any good?

1647
01:00:09,140 --> 01:00:10,575
Yes, he became normal.

1648
01:00:10,608 --> 01:00:11,808
Except for size.

1649
01:00:11,842 --> 01:00:13,377
He was huge, but normal.

1650
01:00:13,410 --> 01:00:14,712
Why weren't
we informed then?

1651
01:00:14,745 --> 01:00:17,048
I could only think of logging
what I'd witnessed.

1652
01:00:17,082 --> 01:00:18,582
All right,
so you logged it.

1653
01:00:18,615 --> 01:00:20,250
Why didn't you
tell us then?

1654
01:00:20,283 --> 01:00:21,852
I don't know.

1655
01:00:21,885 --> 01:00:23,553
I was trying to help.

1656
01:00:23,587 --> 01:00:25,657
I was just trying to help.

1657
01:00:25,690 --> 01:00:26,890
Help?

1658
01:00:26,923 --> 01:00:28,827
You've jeopardized
this whole project.

1659
01:00:28,860 --> 01:00:31,162
What the hell do you
want from me, Dr. Brent?

1660
01:00:31,195 --> 01:00:32,997
I don't have a precision mind
like yours.

1661
01:00:33,030 --> 01:00:35,667
- I'm only a scientist!
- Fine, I made a mistake.

1662
01:00:35,700 --> 01:00:38,703
But the fact is, frank Douglas
is gone with the antidote.

1663
01:00:38,737 --> 01:00:41,372
Hey, you came
into my l ab, you know?

1664
01:00:41,405 --> 01:00:44,275
You're the jerk.

1665
01:00:44,308 --> 01:00:47,112
Huzzah!

1666
01:00:47,145 --> 01:00:48,345
Go ahead, spank me.

1667
01:00:48,378 --> 01:00:50,715
Chalk me up
as incompetent.

1668
01:00:50,748 --> 01:00:52,417
Well...
It's all right.

1669
01:00:52,450 --> 01:00:53,852
We can laugh about it now,
buddy.

1670
01:00:53,885 --> 01:00:55,085
We're both a little bit edgy.

1671
01:00:55,118 --> 01:00:56,920
I was just thinking
one mistake can be...

1672
01:00:56,953 --> 01:00:58,790
[Laughing]
Boy, I can't stay mad at you.

1673
01:00:58,823 --> 01:01:01,492
We better call...

1674
01:01:01,525 --> 01:01:02,493
It's the group.

1675
01:01:02,527 --> 01:01:05,295
- Oh.
- [Humming]

1676
01:01:05,328 --> 01:01:06,997
I'm in love, Jim!

1677
01:01:07,032 --> 01:01:08,867
[Birds tweeting]

1678
01:01:08,900 --> 01:01:13,070
This is like "vanity fair"
by Sam peckinpah.

1679
01:01:13,103 --> 01:01:14,972
Hmm.

1680
01:01:15,005 --> 01:01:17,342
Whoa, that guy's
got a weird shape.

1681
01:01:17,375 --> 01:01:18,408
- No, crow, it's a--
- oh.

1682
01:01:18,442 --> 01:01:20,077
Now, that's science.

1683
01:01:20,110 --> 01:01:21,745
- Oh.
- Connect the dots.

1684
01:01:21,778 --> 01:01:23,180
Science!

1685
01:01:25,248 --> 01:01:29,820
Duh, hello, ladies,
which way to the beach?

1686
01:01:29,853 --> 01:01:32,557
Jeez, he's kind of
a lumpy fella.

1687
01:01:32,590 --> 01:01:33,858
Hmm, in all the wrong places.

1688
01:01:33,892 --> 01:01:35,125
[Pinging]

1689
01:01:35,158 --> 01:01:37,595
Duracell,
the copper top battery.

1690
01:01:37,628 --> 01:01:40,230
Hi, I'm from the boy's camp
across the lake.

1691
01:01:40,263 --> 01:01:42,600
A backup generator exploded.

1692
01:01:42,633 --> 01:01:45,068
[Pinging continues]

1693
01:01:50,107 --> 01:01:51,075
Is it the bangles?

1694
01:01:51,142 --> 01:01:52,110
The go-go's?

1695
01:01:52,143 --> 01:01:53,577
The lark quartet?

1696
01:01:58,917 --> 01:02:00,350
Well...

1697
01:02:00,383 --> 01:02:03,053
- [All screaming]
- The Beatles!

1698
01:02:03,087 --> 01:02:05,155
The is the "a-go go" part
here.

1699
01:02:05,188 --> 01:02:08,960
No one's going
to tell you need clearasil.

1700
01:02:08,993 --> 01:02:11,495
Damn, damn, stupid, stupid,
what was I thinking?

1701
01:02:11,528 --> 01:02:12,630
Oh, damn...

1702
01:02:12,663 --> 01:02:15,633
Hey, a whole bottle
of sea & ski!

1703
01:02:19,670 --> 01:02:22,807
- Unbelievable.
- [Laughing]

1704
01:02:22,840 --> 01:02:24,742
General Connors?

1705
01:02:24,775 --> 01:02:25,943
Yes?

1706
01:02:25,977 --> 01:02:27,112
I made that phone noise.

1707
01:02:27,145 --> 01:02:28,112
Where?

1708
01:02:36,253 --> 01:02:37,222
They panicked.

1709
01:02:37,255 --> 01:02:38,355
Are you saying
it was my fault?

1710
01:02:38,388 --> 01:02:40,023
Was anyone seriously hurt?

1711
01:02:40,057 --> 01:02:41,692
No, thank god.

1712
01:02:41,725 --> 01:02:45,162
Well, he's got to be stopped
before anything else happens.

1713
01:02:45,195 --> 01:02:47,532
- Washington called.
- He did?

1714
01:02:47,565 --> 01:02:50,000
They put me
completely in charge.

1715
01:02:50,033 --> 01:02:53,270
Now, they want results,
and they want 'em fast.

1716
01:02:53,303 --> 01:02:55,372
I need to know some answers.

1717
01:02:55,405 --> 01:02:57,175
Please phrase it
in the form of a question.

1718
01:02:57,208 --> 01:02:58,810
Is there anything else
I should know?

1719
01:02:58,843 --> 01:03:00,545
- Are your flies open?
- I'm doing all I can.

1720
01:03:00,578 --> 01:03:01,712
It's not enough.

1721
01:03:01,745 --> 01:03:03,013
I don't need you
busting my butt.

1722
01:03:03,047 --> 01:03:04,182
They want answers,
they want 'em now.

1723
01:03:04,215 --> 01:03:05,750
Now, don't mince words.

1724
01:03:05,783 --> 01:03:09,753
All right, but understand,
this is just a theory.

1725
01:03:09,787 --> 01:03:12,055
But I'm coco Chanel!

1726
01:03:12,088 --> 01:03:14,725
Douglas
is much worse.

1727
01:03:14,758 --> 01:03:16,460
And?

1728
01:03:16,493 --> 01:03:19,363
And if my theory is correct,

1729
01:03:19,397 --> 01:03:21,265
he gets
progressively worse.

1730
01:03:21,298 --> 01:03:22,433
Quit kidding a kidder.

1731
01:03:22,467 --> 01:03:23,667
What do you mean?

1732
01:03:23,700 --> 01:03:26,603
Well, when I first hid him
in the other lab,

1733
01:03:26,637 --> 01:03:28,405
I gave him an injection
of the antidote

1734
01:03:28,438 --> 01:03:29,840
my brother
and Nora developed.

1735
01:03:29,873 --> 01:03:31,775
John Lilly called
for the prescription.

1736
01:03:31,808 --> 01:03:34,045
It worked, and he
became almost normal.

1737
01:03:34,078 --> 01:03:37,315
But the condition
lasted only five days.

1738
01:03:37,348 --> 01:03:39,483
- Then...
- He left for the weekend.

1739
01:03:39,517 --> 01:03:40,852
... with each following
injection,

1740
01:03:40,885 --> 01:03:43,820
the effect of the antidote
wore off more rapidly.

1741
01:03:43,855 --> 01:03:45,590
What are you trying to say?

1742
01:03:45,623 --> 01:03:46,957
I'm coco Chanel!

1743
01:03:46,990 --> 01:03:50,695
The last injection was effective
for only a few hours.

1744
01:03:50,728 --> 01:03:51,762
Are you trying
to tell me--

1745
01:03:51,795 --> 01:03:53,630
- precisely.
- There's your punch.

1746
01:03:53,663 --> 01:03:55,398
It means, colonel, that
if he is using the antidote

1747
01:03:55,432 --> 01:03:58,102
he escaped with
as his body needs it...

1748
01:03:58,135 --> 01:03:59,770
Hmm.

1749
01:03:59,803 --> 01:04:02,005
There is no telling
how bad he will get

1750
01:04:02,038 --> 01:04:03,407
after each
injection wears off.

1751
01:04:03,440 --> 01:04:05,108
- Good read.
- That's a wrap.

1752
01:04:05,142 --> 01:04:07,578
No, he doesn't get larger.

1753
01:04:07,612 --> 01:04:10,147
The radius
of his danger zone does.

1754
01:04:10,180 --> 01:04:11,648
Isn't that always the way,
though?

1755
01:04:11,682 --> 01:04:13,650
Perhaps he doesn't
have any antidote.

1756
01:04:13,683 --> 01:04:16,753
I'm not sure,
but it's conceivable...

1757
01:04:16,787 --> 01:04:17,788
Inconceivable!

1758
01:04:17,822 --> 01:04:18,923
That within hours,

1759
01:04:18,957 --> 01:04:20,858
he could contaminate
everything and everybody

1760
01:04:20,892 --> 01:04:22,960
within a radius of 50 miles.

1761
01:04:22,993 --> 01:04:25,228
That settles it.

1762
01:04:25,262 --> 01:04:28,132
- Uhh, sausage burp.
- He has got to be destroyed.

1763
01:04:28,165 --> 01:04:30,133
Alert the entire division.

1764
01:04:30,167 --> 01:04:33,537
Civilian forces,
anything else that's available.

1765
01:04:33,570 --> 01:04:35,707
He's got to be kept out
of populated areas.

1766
01:04:35,740 --> 01:04:37,040
Or it'll be bonkers.

1767
01:04:37,073 --> 01:04:38,242
I want you with me
every minute.

1768
01:04:38,275 --> 01:04:40,712
[Giggling]
Yes, sir, thank you, sir.

1769
01:04:42,747 --> 01:04:45,683
[All]
♪ John Jacob
jingleheimer schmidt ♪

1770
01:04:45,717 --> 01:04:48,618
♪ that's my name too ♪

1771
01:04:48,652 --> 01:04:51,022
♪ whenever I go out,
the people always shout ♪

1772
01:04:51,055 --> 01:04:53,157
♪ there goes John Jacob
jingleheimer schmidt ♪

1773
01:04:53,190 --> 01:04:54,325
♪ la-la-la-la-la-la-la ♪
[Laughing]

1774
01:04:54,358 --> 01:04:55,893
Oh, that was fun.

1775
01:04:55,927 --> 01:04:57,828
Ah, thanks, Kyle.

1776
01:04:57,862 --> 01:04:59,930
As the giant became bolder,

1777
01:04:59,963 --> 01:05:02,533
his whereabouts
were more easily tracked,

1778
01:05:02,567 --> 01:05:05,102
and the army was called out
in force.

1779
01:05:05,135 --> 01:05:07,137
Because this was,
after all,

1780
01:05:07,170 --> 01:05:08,138
an American astronaut...

1781
01:05:08,172 --> 01:05:09,240
Oh, right.

1782
01:05:09,273 --> 01:05:11,708
Official orders
were not to fire.

1783
01:05:11,742 --> 01:05:14,345
